window.ScienceStudioLessons = [{"number":1,"title":"Fallen tree. New life.","question":"A fallen tree can become a neighborhood. How?","story":"We’re starting with a fallen tree that looks anything but empty. Give students time to notice what’s living there and sketch how it might be connected to the log. Their first models and questions give us a reason to zoom in next time.","science":"","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 01\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nA fallen tree can become a neighborhood.\nHow?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 01\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nWe’re starting with a fallen tree that looks anything but empty. Give students time to notice what’s living\nthere and sketch how it might be connected to the log. Their first models and questions give us a reason\nto zoom in next time.\nHere is the mystery. Open the unit by letting students meet the phenomenon: a fallen tree with other\nthings growing on it. They need time to notice what is visible and wonder about what they cannot see.\n1. Make observations | 30 min. Show the photos and redwood clip. Collect observations and questions\nbefore introducing the term nurse log.\n2. Develop a model | 30 min. Have students sketch how a fallen tree might support other organisms.\nCompare models and mark ideas that need evidence.\n3. Identify related phenomena | 20 min. Invite examples from students’ communities. Ask what makes\neach example similar to the nurse log.\n4. Ask questions | 30 min. Cluster student questions on the Driving Question Board. Have students\npropose ways to investigate them.\nWhere it leads. Save the models and questions for Lesson 2. The class will take a closer look and start\nnaming the parts of this system.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 50–52","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 01\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Open the Lesson 1 deck and queue the redwood clip. Make four chart spaces for each class. For 22\nstudents, prepare six large sheets for Connect and about 8–11 Investigation Ideas sheets for pairs or\ntrios.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Explore a New Phenomenon • Initial Model • Related Phenomena • sticky note • marker\nPer small group:\n• large (at least 11x17 inches) piece of paper • markers • Investigation Ideas\nPer class:\n• chart paper • chart paper marker • chart paper markers • Notice and Wonder chart • Initial Consensus\nModel • Related Phenomena chart\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 52 (PDF viewer p. 58).\n\nPREP DESK  /  LESSON 01\nLesson 1 | Print and set up\nPrint one teacher copy of the companion. For each class, print about 22 sets of individual handout PDF\npages 2–5. Print group handout PDF page 2 once per pair or trio for Investigation Ideas. Adjust to actual\nenrollment. Students can use a notebook T-chart for Notice and Wonder. Open the official Lesson 1\ndeck. Queue the 19-second Fallen Redwood Tree clip for slide 9. The embedded player appeared blank\nduring preview. The YouTube copy has no captions. Use the visual transcript on Teacher Guide printed\npage 100 to prepare a description. Check classroom playback. If the clip is unavailable, use the five\nphotos on slide 8 and record observations from the photos. Set up four spaces for each class: Notice\nand Wonder, Initial Consensus Model, Related Phenomena, and the Driving Question Board. Set out\nmarkers, sticky notes, and six large sheets for groups of three or four.","resume":null,"earlyResume":"PAUSE / RESUME  /  LESSON 01\nStop and restart\nBLOCK 1\nFinish Explore. Begin individual models if time allows. Save Notice and Wonder and each\nstudent model. Restart at the unfinished M or N step, or at O when models are ready.\nBLOCK 2\nFinish Synthesize and work through Connect as time allows. Save the consensus model and\nrelated phenomena. Finish Connect next time if needed, then open U.\nBLOCK 3\nFinish Navigate. Save each class Driving Question Board and model. Begin Lesson 2 with one\nquestion that closer observation could answer.","stages":[{"title":"Notice and wonder","ref":"Deck position 2","minutes":"Explore · 30 min total","move":"Give students a T-chart. Ask them to write or sketch one thing they see and one thing they wonder. Invite labels in any language.","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A · deck position 2","title":"Explore a new phenomenon","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p2#slide=id.p2","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Give students a T-chart for one observation and one question.","ask":"What do you notice about this fallen tree?","listen":"A visible detail and a question that goes beyond it."},"source":"56","ask":"Which part could someone else point to in the picture?","listen":"If students start with an explanation, invite them to record the visible clue under Notice and turn the explanation into a Wonder.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 3–7 · Look closely at the photos."},{"title":"Look closely at the photos","ref":"Deck position 3–7","minutes":"Explore · 30 min total","move":"Display the photographs one at a time. Ask, “Where do you see that?” On slide 6, ask how the people help students judge the size of the log.","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B · deck position 3","title":"Make observations · photo","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p3#slide=id.p3","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C · deck position 4","title":"Make observations · photo","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p4#slide=id.p4","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D · deck position 5","title":"Make observations · photo","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p5#slide=id.p5","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E · deck position 6","title":"Make observations · photo","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p6#slide=id.p6","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F · deck position 7","title":"Make observations · photo","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p7#slide=id.p7","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7}],"cue":{"do":"Show the photos one at a time. Use people in the image for scale.","ask":"Where do you see that?","listen":"A student points to a specific feature in a photo."},"source":"56–58","ask":"What in the picture gives you a sense of the tree’s size?","listen":"If students call it small because it fits on the screen, ask them to compare it with people or other familiar objects in the image.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 8–9 · Compare photos and video."},{"title":"Compare photos and video","ref":"Deck position 8–9","minutes":"Explore · 30 min total","move":"Use slide 8 to compare the five photos and slide 9 to observe the fallen redwood. Ask students what they noticed on this second look and what remains hard to see.","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"G","label":"Slide G · deck position 8","title":"Make observations · photo collection","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p8#slide=id.p8","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H · deck position 9","title":"Video of a fallen redwood tree","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p9#slide=id.p9","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9}],"cue":{"do":"Use the five-photo collection and the redwood clip to take a second look.","ask":"What did the second look reveal that we missed at first?","listen":"A new observation and something the sources still cannot show."},"source":"58","ask":"Which detail could you see in the photos, and which did you see in the video?","listen":"If a student says the video shows how plants arrived, replay the relevant moment and separate the tree falling from predictions about later growth.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 10–12 · Share and record."},{"title":"Share and record","ref":"Deck position 10–12","minutes":"Explore · 30 min total","move":"Have groups rehearse one observation and one question. Record both on the class chart. If a student says, “The log feeds the plant,” ask, “What do you see, and what are you proposing might happen?”","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"I","label":"Slide I · deck position 10","title":"Share observations and questions · group","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p10#slide=id.p10","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J · deck position 11","title":"Share observations and questions · class","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p11#slide=id.p11","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K · deck position 12","title":"Share observations and questions · discussion prompts","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p12#slide=id.p12","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Have groups rehearse an observation and a question. Record them on the class chart.","ask":"What do you see, and what are you proposing might happen?","listen":"Students distinguish an observation from an explanation."},"source":"58–60","ask":"Which classmate’s observation connects to your question?","listen":"If only a few voices are shaping the chart, invite partners to share an idea they rehearsed and record it in their own words.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 13 · Name the nurse log."},{"title":"Name the nurse log","ref":"Deck position 13","minutes":"Explore · 30 min total","move":"Introduce “nurse log” after students have shared their observations. Save the Notice and Wonder chart. Resume with slide 14 when Explore is complete.","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"L","label":"Slide L · deck position 13","title":"Scientists’ name for the phenomenon","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p13#slide=id.p13","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13}],"cue":{"do":"Introduce the term nurse log after students share observations. Save the chart.","ask":"Which observation made you wonder how the fallen tree helps other living things?","listen":"A connection to an observed plant, fungus, or other feature."},"source":"61","ask":"What does the name nurse log make you wonder about the fallen tree?","listen":"If students treat the name as an explanation, return to one visible feature and leave how the log helps living things as a question.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 14 · Introduce scientific models."},{"title":"Introduce scientific models","ref":"Deck position 14","minutes":"Synthesize · 30 min total","move":"Use the slide’s shadow example. Ask what the model helps explain.","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"M","label":"Slide M · deck position 14","title":"What is modeling?","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p14#slide=id.p14","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14}],"cue":{"do":"Use the publisher’s shadow example to discuss what a model can show.","ask":"What does this model help us explain?","listen":"A model represents an idea or relationship, beyond a picture of the object."},"source":"63","ask":"What could a model show that the photograph alone cannot?","listen":"If students think a model must copy the picture, invite them to show one proposed interaction with a label or arrow.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 15 · Make an initial model."},{"title":"Make an initial model","ref":"Deck position 15","minutes":"Synthesize · 30 min total","move":"Use both Initial Model pages. Have students show what may happen on, under, and inside the log. Invite arrows and labels. Use question marks for uncertain ideas.","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"N","label":"Slide N · deck position 15","title":"Develop a model","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p15#slide=id.p15","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15}],"cue":{"do":"Use both Initial Model pages. Invite arrows, labels, and question marks.","ask":"What might be happening on, under, or inside the log?","listen":"A proposed relationship and a way to mark an uncertain idea."},"source":"63","ask":"Which part of your model is an idea you want to test?","listen":"If a drawing shows organisms without a connection, ask how one might use the log and let the student mark uncertainty.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 16–17 · Compare partner models."},{"title":"Compare partner models","ref":"Deck position 16–17","minutes":"Synthesize · 30 min total","move":"Gather in a Scientists Circle and revisit the class agreements. Have partners explain two similarities and two differences between their models.","id":8,"slides":[{"id":"O","label":"Slide O · deck position 16","title":"Gather in a Scientists Circle","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p16#slide=id.p16","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16},{"id":"P","label":"Slide P · deck position 17","title":"Share models","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p17#slide=id.p17","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17}],"cue":{"do":"Revisit class agreements in the Scientists Circle. Have partners compare similarities and differences.","ask":"Where do your models explain the same thing differently?","listen":"A specific comparison between the two models."},"source":"64–65","ask":"Where do the two models make different predictions?","listen":"If partners only compare how their drawings look, have each explain one relationship and identify a difference in their thinking.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 18–19 · Build a class model."},{"title":"Build a class model","ref":"Deck position 18–19","minutes":"Synthesize · 30 min total","move":"Ask which ideas can go on the model now and which need evidence. If students disagree about whether roots reach through the log, record both possibilities. Ask what a closer observation could reveal.","id":9,"slides":[{"id":"Q","label":"Slide Q · deck position 18","title":"Develop an initial consensus model","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p18#slide=id.p18","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-q.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":18},{"id":"R","label":"Slide R · deck position 19","title":"Develop an initial consensus model · discussion prompts","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p19#slide=id.p19","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-r.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":19}],"cue":{"do":"Collect possible relationships. Keep competing ideas visible and save the individual models.","ask":"Which idea needs more evidence before we can agree?","listen":"A proposed relationship and an observation that could test it."},"source":"65–66","ask":"Which idea can we represent together, and which still needs evidence?","listen":"If the group disagrees about a link, mark the uncertainty on the model and invite both explanations.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 20 · Invite a community example."},{"title":"Invite a community example","ref":"Deck position 20","minutes":"Connect · 20 min total","move":"Ask students to look for something that reminds them of the nurse log. They can talk with a family or community member and bring back an example if able.","id":10,"slides":[{"id":"S","label":"Slide S · deck position 20","title":"Connect to our communities","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p20#slide=id.p20","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-s.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":20}],"cue":{"do":"Ask students to look for something in their community that reminds them of the nurse log.","ask":"Where else have you seen one thing providing a place for other living things?","listen":"A familiar example students can describe from experience."},"source":"70","ask":"Where else have you seen something living on or near something that was once alive?","listen":"If a student names a place only, invite a sketch or description of what was there and how it resembles the log.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 21 · Compare community examples."},{"title":"Compare community examples","ref":"Deck position 21","minutes":"Connect · 20 min total","move":"Give groups large paper. Ask, “What is similar to the nurse log? What is different?” Record the connections students explain. Save the chart and resume at slide 22 if class ends here.","id":11,"slides":[{"id":"T","label":"Slide T · deck position 21","title":"Identify related phenomena","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p21#slide=id.p21","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-t.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":21}],"cue":{"do":"Use large paper to record the connections each group explains.","ask":"What is similar to the nurse log? What is different?","listen":"A comparison based on a relationship, not just appearance."},"source":"70","ask":"What do these two examples have in common besides both being outdoors?","listen":"If the connection is broad, ask the group to name a specific feature or relationship they observed in both examples.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 22 · Write investigation questions."},{"title":"Write investigation questions","ref":"Deck position 22","minutes":"Navigate · 30 min total","move":"Reopen the charts and model. Have students write one question per sticky note.","id":12,"slides":[{"id":"U","label":"Slide U · deck position 22","title":"Ask questions","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p22#slide=id.p22","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-u.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":22}],"cue":{"do":"Reopen the charts and model. Have students write one question per sticky note.","ask":"Which part of our model are you still wondering about?","listen":"A question tied to a model feature or observation."},"source":"71","ask":"Which detail from your drawing or our chart made you ask that?","listen":"If students struggle to start a question, have them point to an observation and say what they want to find out about it.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 23 · Build the Driving Question Board."},{"title":"Build the Driving Question Board","ref":"Deck position 23","minutes":"Navigate · 30 min total","move":"Ask students to read their questions and explain which ones belong together. Keep distinct questions visible within each cluster.","id":13,"slides":[{"id":"V","label":"Slide V · deck position 23","title":"Organize our questions","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p23#slide=id.p23","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-v.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":23}],"cue":{"do":"Have students read and cluster their questions. Keep distinct questions visible.","ask":"Why do these questions belong together?","listen":"A shared idea that explains the grouping."},"source":"71–72","ask":"Whose question is related to yours, and what makes them related?","listen":"If questions are grouped by a shared word alone, ask students to explain what the questions are trying to find out.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 24 · Propose a way to investigate."},{"title":"Propose a way to investigate","ref":"Deck position 24","minutes":"Navigate · 30 min total","move":"Have pairs or trios choose a question and suggest something the class could observe. Ask how that observation would help.","id":14,"slides":[{"id":"W","label":"Slide W · deck position 24","title":"Brainstorm ideas for investigation","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p24#slide=id.p24","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-w.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":24}],"cue":{"do":"Have pairs choose a question and propose something the class could observe.","ask":"How would that observation help answer your question?","listen":"A connection between an observable feature and the question."},"source":"73","ask":"What would you look for, and where could you collect that evidence?","listen":"If a pair says only “look it up,” ask what information they would seek or what an observation could show.","transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Deck position 25 · Choose the next investigation."},{"title":"Choose the next investigation","ref":"Deck position 25","minutes":"Navigate · 30 min total","move":"Use a student question to motivate taking a closer look at a nurse log in Lesson 2. Save each class’s board and model.","id":15,"slides":[{"id":"X","label":"Slide X · deck position 25","title":"Consider where to go next","url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit?slide=id.p25#slide=id.p25","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-01-x.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 1 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":25}],"cue":{"do":"Use a class question to motivate a closer look in Lesson 2. Save each class’s board and model.","ask":"Which of our questions could a closer look help us answer?","listen":"A question that could be investigated by observing the log."},"source":"73","ask":"Which question could a closer look at the log help us answer?","listen":"If several directions are possible, point to the class question board and select a question students can investigate with the available evidence.","transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":4,"prep":5,"briefing":7,"resume":8,"teaching":11,"teachingEnd":13,"field":14,"checklist":6},"access":"","evidence":"Look for a named observation and a question it motivates. In models, separate observed details from proposed connections.","checklist":["Open the official slides and queue the redwood clip; verify playback before class.","Set out Notice and Wonder, Initial Consensus Model, Related Phenomena, and Driving Question Board spaces.","Prepare individual pages, one Investigation Ideas sheet per pair or trio, and large paper for Connect groups.","Gather sticky notes, markers, and student notebooks."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/14r4y6mcGd2zeiTMt_aV_P-T9CsnPtFBo34IpTFSMfWM/edit","label":"Open Lesson 1 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-01.jpg"},{"number":2,"title":"Inside the nurse log","question":"Zoom in. What lives in, on, and around the log?","story":"Last time we saw a fallen tree supporting other life. Now we’re looking closer, using different sources to find what lives in, on, and around the log. The questions students raise about moss will help launch our investigation of plant growth.","science":"","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 02\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nZoom in. What lives in, on, and around the\nlog?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 02\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nLast time we saw a fallen tree supporting other life. Now we’re looking closer, using different sources to\nfind what lives in, on, and around the log. The questions students raise about moss will help launch our\ninvestigation of plant growth.\nHere is the mystery. Use a question from Lesson 1 to give the station work a purpose. Students will\ncompare what different sources let them see.\n1. Take stock of our questions | 5 min. Revisit the class questions.\n2. Make observations | 20 min. Rotate through the nurse log stations and record what each source\nshows.\n3. Participate in a discussion | 10 min. Add visible components to the model and mark guesses\nseparately.\n4. Connect to our communities | 10 min. Connect the observations to things students might see in\ntheir community.\n5. Plan to explore | 10 min. Turn a wondering about moss into a testable question.\n6. Make observations | 10 min. Use hand lenses to compare moss features.\n7. Participate in a discussion | 10 min. Ask students to point to the features behind their claims.\n8. Create a My Growing Ideas chart | 10 min. Record an idea and its evidence in My Growing Ideas.\n9. Revisit our Driving Question Board | 5 min. Return to the Driving Question Board and add\nquestions about plants.\nWhere it leads. Those questions about moss give Lesson 3 a reason to investigate how plants grow.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 101–103","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 02\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Prepare nurse log, picture and video stations. For six groups, stage two of each type if two terraria are\navailable. Prepare moss, dishes and lenses for eight groups. Read printed pp. 127–128 for rotations,\ncare and cleanup.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Explore a Nurse Log • Nurse Log Components in Our Community • My Growing Ideas\nPer partners:\n• Observe Moss • small piece of moss • hand lens • Observe Moss\nPer small group:\n• Explore a Nurse Log • hand lens\nPer class:\n• Explore a Nurse Log • prepared Nurse Log in a Box • Nurse Log Pictures • and Nurse Log Videos\nstations as described in Nurse Log & Moss Stations Setup • Driving Question Board • initial consensus\nmodel from Lesson 1 • “Nurse Log Components in Our Community” chart paper • “Our Questions About\nMoss” chart paper\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 104 (PDF viewer p. 110).\n\nPREP DESK  /  LESSON 02\nPrint and set up\nLesson 2 | What things are found in, on, and around nurse logs?\nPRINT\nPrint one teacher copy single-sided. Student Bundle PDF viewer pages: Explore a Nurse Log 19-20 (22\nsets/class), Observe Moss 21-22 (8 group sets or 11 partner sets/class), My Growing Ideas 23\n(22/class), Community Connection 133-134 (22 sets/class if sending home). Adjust to enrollment.\nTeacher Guide PDF pp. 136-141: two color sets for picture stations.\nPREPARE THE LOG STATIONS\nAllow the publisher's 60 minutes of preparation. For 22 students, make six groups: four groups of four\nand two of three. If two terraria are available, set up two live, two photo and two video stations. Start two\ngroups at each type. Rotate in three five-minute rounds, with five minutes for directions and transitions.\nWith one live station, arrange extra time or omit that station as the publisher allows.\nPREPARE MOSS\nFor the publisher's eight-group setup, plan six trios and two pairs. Stage samples, dishes and hand\nlenses. Organize four rounds so each group sees each moss type. Return moss to its tank and maintain\nit through at least Lesson 7. Keep Lesson 9 isopods separate from the nurse log kit.\nSAFETY AND ACCESS\nFollow vendor care sheets and school procedures. Use eyes and hand lenses at the live-log station and\nkeep the lid on between groups. If handling is permitted, review the publisher's precautions on printed p.\n109 and its handwashing guidance. Offer sketches, oral descriptions and partner rehearsal.\nOPEN BEFORE CLASS\nOpen the official Lesson 2 deck and queue the four publisher videos linked in Science Studio. Verify\nplayback on the classroom device. Reuse station materials for the second class and keep each class's\ncharts separate.","resume":null,"earlyResume":"PAUSE / RESUME  /  LESSON 02\nStop and restart\nBLOCK 1\nComplete A-F. Save the station observations, model and community chart. Restart at G with\nwhat students noticed about moss.\nBLOCK 2\nComplete G-R. Save observations and testable questions. Open Lesson 3 with a student\nquestion about moss or plants.","stages":[{"title":"Reopen the Driving Question Board","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"5 min","move":"Reopen the Lesson 1 Driving Question Board. Choose an actual class question a closer look could help answer. Ask what students would need to see.","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Choose a class question that a closer look at the log could help answer.","ask":"What would we need to see to answer this?","listen":"A proposed observation linked to the question."},"source":"108","ask":"Which question might we make progress on by looking more closely?","listen":"If students bring up a question about a long-term change, keep it on the board and help them identify a feature they can observe today.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B · Observe the nurse log."},{"title":"Observe the nurse log","ref":"Slides B","minutes":"20 min","move":"Rotate through live log, pictures and video. Ask students to label their observation by source and location: in, on or around. Possible observation: Green patches cover part of the wood. Ask where they are and which source shows them.","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3}],"cue":{"do":"Rotate through the live log, images, and video as available. Label each observation by source and location.","ask":"Where is that feature, and which source shows it?","listen":"A detail located in, on, or around the log."},"source":"109–110","ask":"Where on the log did you see it, and what did it look like?","listen":"If students name an organism they cannot identify yet, have them describe its appearance and location and keep the name provisional.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides C-D · Update the model with observations."},{"title":"Update the model with observations","ref":"Slides C-D","minutes":"10 min","move":"Add observed components to the initial consensus model. If a student says an animal must live inside, ask what they saw and what would count as evidence. Mark predictions with a question mark. Introduce the system idea by asking how the parts might relate.","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5}],"cue":{"do":"Add observed components to the model. Mark predictions with a question mark.","ask":"What did we see that supports adding this component?","listen":"Observed features separated from possible unseen organisms."},"source":"111–114","ask":"Which part of our model would you add or change based on what we saw?","listen":"If students propose a connection without evidence, add the observed component and mark the proposed relationship with a question mark.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides E-F · Connect to the community."},{"title":"Connect to the community","ref":"Slides E-F","minutes":"10 min","move":"Invite students to look for familiar components in their community. Save the community chart and model. If class ends here, resume with slide G and the class's questions about moss.","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7}],"cue":{"do":"Have students name familiar components from their community. Save the chart and model.","ask":"What have you seen near home that reminds you of this part of the log?","listen":"A comparison with a named feature or relationship."},"source":"115–116","ask":"What did you see in your community that resembles one part of our log system?","listen":"If students did not make an outside observation, invite them to compare a classmate’s example with the nurse-log evidence.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides G-J · Turn moss wonderings into questions."},{"title":"Turn moss wonderings into questions","ref":"Slides G-J","minutes":"10 min","move":"Revisit what students noticed about moss. Help them turn a broad wondering into a question the class can investigate. Ask what they could actually observe.","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11}],"cue":{"do":"Revisit the moss observations. Help students narrow a broad wondering into a testable question.","ask":"What could we actually observe to answer that?","listen":"A question that names a feature students can compare."},"source":"117–118","ask":"What could we observe or compare to make progress on that question?","listen":"If a question is too broad for the available materials, help students narrow it to one visible feature or comparison.","block":2,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides K-M · Compare moss samples."},{"title":"Compare moss samples","ref":"Slides K-M","minutes":"10 min","move":"Invite an initial prediction about moss. Ask partners what feature would help them decide. Compare samples using hand lenses and record visible features on Observe Moss.","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13},{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14}],"cue":{"do":"Have partners predict a feature, then use hand lenses and record what they see.","ask":"Which feature would help you compare these samples?","listen":"An observed feature described or sketched on Observe Moss."},"source":"120–121","ask":"Which moss feature would help you decide what group it belongs in?","listen":"If students vote based only on color, invite a closer look at structures and ask them to record what they see before revising their claim.","block":2,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides N-O · Discuss evidence about moss."},{"title":"Discuss evidence about moss","ref":"Slides N-O","minutes":"10 min","move":"Have students point to or sketch the feature behind their claim. If someone says green means it is a plant, ask which other features they can compare. Keep unresolved ideas visible for later evidence.","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15},{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Have students point to or sketch the feature behind their claim.","ask":"What feature besides color could help us compare?","listen":"A claim supported by an observed feature rather than color alone."},"source":"122–123","ask":"Which observed feature supports or challenges your first idea?","listen":"If students describe the wispy structures as ordinary roots, compare them with the plant image and introduce rhizoids in the context of what students observed.","block":2,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides P · Record Growing Ideas."},{"title":"Record Growing Ideas","ref":"Slides P","minutes":"10 min","move":"Have students record one thing they figured out and the observation that supports it. Use drawings, labels or words.","id":8,"slides":[{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17}],"cue":{"do":"Have students draw, label, or write one new idea and its supporting observation.","ask":"What did you figure out, and which observation supports it?","listen":"An idea paired with evidence from the investigation."},"source":"124","ask":"What did you figure out, and how did you figure it out?","listen":"If a chart entry only says “moss is a plant,” have the student add one observation or sketch that supported their thinking.","block":2,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides Q-R · Carry questions forward."},{"title":"Carry questions forward","ref":"Slides Q-R","minutes":"5 min","move":"Revisit the Driving Question Board. Add new questions about moss and plant growth. Carry those questions into Lesson 3.","id":9,"slides":[{"id":"Q","label":"Slide Q","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-q.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":18},{"id":"R","label":"Slide R","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-02-r.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 2 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":19}],"cue":{"do":"Add unresolved moss and plant-growth questions to the Driving Question Board.","ask":"Which question should we carry into the plant investigation?","listen":"A question about plant growth the class can investigate."},"source":"125–126","ask":"What would we need to find out about plants to explain where moss grows?","listen":"If a question revisits a settled observation, ask students to build on it by naming a condition they could investigate.","block":2,"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":15,"prep":16,"briefing":18,"resume":19,"teaching":21,"teachingEnd":22,"field":23,"checklist":17},"access":"","evidence":"Look for one observation labeled by source and location. During Observe Moss, ask students to point to a feature that supports the claim.","checklist":["Prepare the nurse-log observation stations: live log, photos, and videos as available.","Stage moss samples, dishes, and hand lenses. Check handling and handwashing guidance.","Copy Explore a Nurse Log and other individual pages. Prepare Observe Moss by group or pair.","Open the lesson deck and media, and keep each class’s station notes and model separate."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1c6bliyiocedFwTLVQ3YhWyjicMkJfIfWzJvdHAAwvBo/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 2 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-02.jpg"},{"number":3,"title":"The pumpkin weigh-in","question":"Big pumpkin! But what does “big” mean?","story":"Our close look at moss raised questions about how plants grow. Before we can test what helps them grow, we need a way to measure growth. Use the pumpkins to surface different ideas about measurement and carry a testable approach into the plant investigation.","science":"","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 03\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nBig pumpkin! But what does “big” mean?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 03\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nOur close look at moss raised questions about how plants grow. Before we can test what helps them\ngrow, we need a way to measure growth. Use the pumpkins to surface different ideas about\nmeasurement and carry a testable approach into the plant investigation.\nHere is the mystery. Lesson 2 raised questions about how plants live and grow on nurse logs. Before\nstudents can compare growth, they need to agree on what to measure.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Revisit a class question about plants.\n2. Connect to our experiences | 15 min. Collect student experiences with growing plants.\n3. Make observations | 10 min. Watch the pumpkin time-lapse and separate visible changes from\nguesses about what caused them.\n4. Carry out an investigation | 20 min. Have groups compare three pumpkins with a consistent\nmethod and units. Ask what each tool measures if groups get different winners.\n5. Read a book | 15 min. Read Scientists Make Arguments and practice asking for evidence.\n6. Evaluate our evidence | 15 min. Ask for a measurement and reasoning that support a claim about\nthe fairest measure.\n7. Ask questions | 10 min. Save testable questions about plant needs.\nWhere it leads. Lesson 4 uses the questions to evaluate an investigation and compare actual plant\ndata.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 139–141","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 03\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Label three similar-sized, differently shaped mini pumpkins A, B and C. Test the scale and five or six\nrulers or tapes. Queue the time-lapse and open Scientists Make Arguments.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Pumpkin Contest • Investigate How to Fairly Measure Pumpkins • Growing Plants • Observe a Giant\nPumpkin Growing • Evaluate the Evidence\nPer small group:\n• stopwatch or timekeeping device (optional)\nPer class:\n• Pumpkin Contest • 3 mini pumpkins or other fruit/vegetable • digital scale • 5-6 measuring tapes or\nrulers • any other available measuring tools • What We Think Plants Need to Grow chart • Giant\nPumpkin Timelapse | aka \"Bear Swipe\" | 2022 All New England Giant Pumpkin Contest Winner video •\nClassroom Agreements chart from Lesson 1 • Scientists Make Arguments book • Classroom\nAgreements chart • chart paper or DQB\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 142 (PDF viewer p. 148).\n\nPREP DESK  /  LESSON 03\nPrint and set up\nLesson 3 | How can we measure how much a plant has grown?\nPRINT\nPrint one teacher copy, single-sided. Student Bundle PDF viewer pages: Growing Plants 25-26,\nObserve a Giant Pumpkin Growing 27-28, Investigate How to Fairly Measure Pumpkins 29-30, and\nEvaluate the Evidence 31-32. Plan about 22 sets of each selected handout per class. The guide allows\nnotebook entries for the first two.\nGATHER\nAllow 45 minutes of prep. Label three similarly sized, differently shaped mini pumpkins A, B and C. The\nguide allows three pieces of the same kind of produce instead. Check the digital scale and five or six\nrulers or tapes. Stage the scale as a shared station and arrange access for about six groups. Reuse\nequipment and produce across classes if suitable.\nOPEN BEFORE CLASS\nHave the official Lesson 3 slides, Pumpkin Contest material, the giant pumpkin time-lapse and\nScientists Make Arguments book or projection ready. The exact media and separate resource links still\nneed confirmation. Bring each class's Lesson 2 questions, Classroom Agreements and a What We\nThink Plants Need to Grow chart.\nSCIENCE IN PLAIN LANGUAGE\nLength, width and weight can rank the same objects differently. Weight lets students compare the\namount of matter in this classroom investigation. Measuring different pumpkins today helps students\nchoose a measure. To find out how much a plant grew, compare measurements of the same plant over\ntime.","resume":null,"earlyResume":"PAUSE / RESUME  /  LESSON 03\nStop and restart\nBLOCK 1\nPause during I-L after students record their procedure and measurements. Save unfinished\ncomparisons. Restart at the unfinished slide.\nBLOCK 2\nFinish I-L, then M-R. Save arguments and testable questions to use when evaluating the\nLesson 4 setup.","stages":[{"title":"Reopen a growth question","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"5 min","move":"Return to a class question about moss or plant growth. Ask what students would need to observe to show how much a plant has grown.","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Choose a question about moss or plant growth from the class board.","ask":"What would we need to observe to show how much a plant has grown?","listen":"An observable change, with the starting and later conditions distinguished."},"source":"146–147","ask":"Which moss question gives us a reason to investigate plant growth?","listen":"If students jump straight to a list of plant needs, return to one moss observation and ask what they still need evidence for.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B-C · Connect to growing experiences."},{"title":"Connect to growing experiences","ref":"Slides B-C","minutes":"15 min","move":"Invite a growing experience. Record ideas about what plants need. If students say water helped, ask what they observed and what they inferred.","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4}],"cue":{"do":"Invite a growing experience and chart ideas about what plants need.","ask":"What did you observe that makes you think that helped the plant grow?","listen":"Students distinguish what they saw from what they inferred."},"source":"147–148","ask":"What did you notice changing in your growing example?","listen":"If stories focus on planting routines alone, invite students to name a change they actually observed and how they noticed it.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides D-H · Observe the pumpkin time-lapse."},{"title":"Observe the pumpkin time-lapse","ref":"Slides D-H","minutes":"10 min","move":"Show the publisher's giant pumpkin time-lapse. Have students record visible changes first. Ask which possible growth factors they could see and what the video leaves open.","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9}],"cue":{"do":"Show the publisher’s time-lapse. Record visible changes before discussing causes.","ask":"Which changes can we see, and what can’t the video tell us?","listen":"A visible change and a growth factor the video cannot isolate."},"source":"149–150","ask":"What can the time-lapse show us, and what would we still need to measure?","listen":"If students treat a visibly bigger pumpkin as proof of a particular weight change, distinguish the visible growth from a measurement.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides I-L · Measure the pumpkins."},{"title":"Measure the pumpkins","ref":"Slides I-L","minutes":"first 15 of 20 min","move":"Ask groups to choose how to judge the biggest pumpkin fairly. Compare all three using the same procedure and units. If a group measures height, ask where they placed the ruler and how another group could repeat the measurement.","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13}],"cue":{"do":"Have groups choose a fair method and use the same procedure and units for all three pumpkins.","ask":"How could another group repeat your measurement?","listen":"A consistent method, a defined starting point, and measurements with units."},"source":"152–153","ask":"How will another group repeat your measurement the same way?","listen":"If partners measure from different starting points, have them agree on a procedure and record the reading with its unit.","block":1,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides I-L · Finish the pumpkin comparison."},{"title":"Finish the pumpkin comparison","ref":"Slides I-L","minutes":"final 5 min","move":"Finish the comparison. If the tallest pumpkin and heaviest pumpkin differ, ask what each tool tells the class. Have students explain which measure best supports a claim about amount of matter.","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13}],"cue":{"do":"Reopen the group measurements. Compare what different tools tell you about the pumpkins.","ask":"Which measure best supports a claim about the amount of matter?","listen":"A distinction between height or size and the measured weight."},"source":"153–155","ask":"Did the two methods rank the pumpkins the same way? What did each method measure?","listen":"If students equate the tallest pumpkin with the heaviest, place the two readings side by side and ask which gives evidence about weight.","block":2,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides M-O · Practice scientific argumentation."},{"title":"Practice scientific argumentation","ref":"Slides M-O","minutes":"15 min","move":"Read Scientists Make Arguments. Use the Classroom Agreements to discuss how students can disagree with a claim and ask for evidence.","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14},{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15},{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Read Scientists Make Arguments and use class agreements to discuss disagreement.","ask":"How can we disagree with a claim and ask for evidence respectfully?","listen":"A response that addresses evidence or reasoning rather than the person."},"source":"156–158","ask":"How could someone disagree with an idea while respecting the person who shared it?","listen":"If a student treats an argument as a quarrel, connect an example from the book to a class agreement and rehearse an evidence-based response.","block":2,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides P-Q · Evaluate the evidence."},{"title":"Evaluate the evidence","ref":"Slides P-Q","minutes":"15 min","move":"Use Evaluate the Evidence. Ask for a claim, a specific measurement with units, and a reason the measurement supports the claim. For claims about plant needs, ask what comparison would help establish whether a factor affected growth.","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17},{"id":"Q","label":"Slide Q","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-q.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":18}],"cue":{"do":"Use Evaluate the Evidence to connect each claim to a specific measurement.","ask":"Which measurement supports your claim, and how?","listen":"A claim, a value with units, and a reason the value matters."},"source":"159–161","ask":"What claim does this evidence support, and what question does it leave open?","listen":"If a student chooses evidence because it agrees with them, ask them to identify what was measured and connect it to the claim.","block":2,"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides R · Save testable plant questions."},{"title":"Save testable plant questions","ref":"Slides R","minutes":"10 min","move":"Add testable questions to the board. Preserve the class's questions for Lesson 4, when students evaluate the plant investigation setup and look at its data.","id":8,"slides":[{"id":"R","label":"Slide R","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-03-r.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 3 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":19}],"cue":{"do":"Add questions to the board and save them for the Lesson 4 setup and data.","ask":"Which question could we test by comparing plants grown under different conditions?","listen":"A testable question that names a condition or comparison."},"source":"162–163","ask":"Which plant growth question could we answer by comparing two conditions?","listen":"If students state an answer as settled, put it into a question and identify the observation or comparison that could test it.","block":2,"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":24,"prep":25,"briefing":27,"resume":28,"teaching":30,"teachingEnd":31,"field":32,"checklist":26},"access":"","evidence":"Look for a repeatable procedure, a measurement with units, and a claim that uses that measurement.","checklist":["Stage three pumpkins and the measuring tools specified in the Prep Desk.","Queue the pumpkin time-lapse and confirm the materials for recording measurements and units.","Copy the assigned handouts and prepare Scientists Make Arguments.","Set out chart paper and preserve each class’s testable questions."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1Ec69y8AJB1cB8SM5cURHPbD-17BCfB2UAqfYfCaLTQI/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 3 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-03.jpg"},{"number":4,"title":"What makes plants grow?","question":"Which plant comparisons give us a fair test?","story":"We’ve chosen a way to measure growth. Now students have to decide which comparisons actually tell us something about growing conditions. Keep the spotlight on the data and what was held constant. Then leave them with the question that drives Lesson 5: Where did the extra matter come from?","science":"Lesson 3 established weight as a way to track growth. Compare changes in the same plant over time and use cases with comparable conditions to reason about what helped it grow. The data do not isolate every possible cause.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 04\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nWhich plant comparisons give us a fair\ntest?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 04\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nWe’ve chosen a way to measure growth. Now students have to decide which comparisons actually tell\nus something about growing conditions. Keep the spotlight on the data and what was held constant.\nThen leave them with the question that drives Lesson 5: Where did the extra matter come from?\nHere is the mystery. Students chose weight as a measure for tracking growth in Lesson 3. Today they\nuse measurements over time and look for comparisons that tell them which conditions mattered.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Return to the questions about plant growth.\n2. Evaluate an investigation | 20 min. Examine the investigation setup before showing the results. Ask\nwhich plants can be compared fairly.\n3. Analyze and interpret data | 35 min. Use the data and graphs to compare changes in weight across\nconditions. Have students name the specific plants behind their claim.\n4. Participate in a discussion | 10 min. Discuss what the comparisons support and where the\nevidence leaves uncertainty.\n5. Read an article | 15 min. Read Digging Into Soil to explore what soil provides.\n6. Decide where to go next | 5 min. Ask where the matter came from when plants gained weight.\nWhere it leads. Lesson 5 investigates whether air, water and light can supply the matter a plant gains.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 169–171","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 04\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Have the Lesson 3 questions ready. Open the investigation setup before displaying plant data or\ngraphs. Prepare the article for the read-aloud.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Investigation Setup • Interpret Plant Data • Digging Into Soil • My Growing Ideas (optional)\nPer small group:\n• Plant Data • Graphs of Plant Data\nPer class:\n• DQB or testable questions list from Lesson 3 • Plant Growth Investigation\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 172 (PDF viewer p. 178).\nBefore class\nG172; S33–49, 51. Sort Investigation Setup and Interpret Plant Data for individual use. Prepare Plant\nData by pair/trio and Graphs of Plant Data for the gallery if not supplied in the kit. Keep data out of view\nuntil after students evaluate the setup. Open Digging Into Soil and retain Lesson 3 questions.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nStudent copies\nOne Investigation Setup (33–36) for G, Interpret Plant Data (43–46) for L, and Digging Into Soil (47–49)\nfor O per student. My Growing Ideas (51) at P is optional if using notebooks.\nGroup and teacher copies\nOne Plant Data set (37–42) per small group. Prepare one Graphs of Plant Data display per assigned\ngallery station from the kit or publisher resource. Hold the results until students evaluate the setup.\nKeep Teacher Key (guide printed pp. 198–200 onward) at the teacher station.","resume":["At a completed J–M comparison, if a pause is needed.","Station number, marked graph, and unfinished claim.","Reopen that evidence before introducing the soil reading."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Reopen testable questions","ref":"Slides A–B","minutes":"5 min","move":"Return to the questions about plant growth.","ask":"Which question from Lesson 3 could this investigation help us answer?","listen":"If students suggest changing everything at once, record the idea and ask which single condition they most want to investigate.","source":"176","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2},{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3}],"cue":{"do":"Bring back the class’s plant-growth questions before showing the investigation setup.","ask":"Which condition did we want to investigate?","listen":"A question about a condition that could be compared."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides C–I · Judge the setup."},{"title":"Judge the setup","ref":"Slides C–I","minutes":"20 min","move":"Show the setup before revealing results. At G, hand out Investigation Setup and have groups record predictions.","ask":"Which conditions differ? What would you keep the same?","listen":"If several variables differ, have students mark both conditions lists and find a more comparable pair. Save the class’s fairness criteria.","source":"177–181","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10}],"cue":{"do":"Show the setup before revealing results. At G, use Investigation Setup to record predictions.","ask":"Which conditions differ, and what should stay the same?","listen":"A comparable pair where the condition of interest differs."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides J–M · Compare plant weights."},{"title":"Compare plant weights","ref":"Slides J–M","minutes":"35 min","move":"Model the first graph comparison at L before students work through the gallery. Have students record starting and ending weights and conditions on Interpret Plant Data.","ask":"Which two readings support the change?","listen":"If students compare final weights alone, have them mark the starting values. Save a marked graph and unfinished claim if the block ends.","source":"182–187","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13},{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14}],"cue":{"do":"Model the first graph comparison at L. Have students record both starting and ending weights.","ask":"Which two readings support the change you described?","listen":"A weight change from two readings, not just the larger final weight."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides N · Discuss claims and limits."},{"title":"Discuss claims and limits","ref":"Slides N","minutes":"10 min","move":"Collect a claim supported by a named comparison. Keep claims visible through the soil reading at O and Growing Ideas at P.","ask":"What can this comparison tell us about soil?","listen":"If students treat healthier growth as proof soil supplied most of the mass, separate the claims. Carry the source-of-matter question to Lesson 5.","source":"188–193","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15}],"cue":{"do":"Collect claims tied to named comparisons. Keep them visible for the soil reading.","ask":"What does this comparison let us claim about soil?","listen":"A claim limited to what the data compare."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides O–P · Read about soil and reflect."},{"title":"Read about soil and reflect","ref":"Slides O–P","minutes":"15 min","move":"Read Digging Into Soil at O. At P, record what students figured out and the evidence that supports it, using Growing Ideas or notebooks.","ask":"What does the soil article help explain that our plant comparison could not?","listen":"If students say the soil supplied all the added plant matter, ask them to distinguish what the reading says about soil from what the weight data show.","source":"191–193","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16},{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17}],"cue":{"do":"Read Digging Into Soil at O. Record evidence and new ideas at P.","ask":"How does the reading help explain the pattern we saw?","listen":"An idea tied to the reading or plant data."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides Q · Leave the matter question open."},{"title":"Leave the matter question open","ref":"Slides Q","minutes":"5 min","move":"Ask where the matter came from when plants gained weight.","ask":"If the plants gained weight, what are we still trying to account for?","listen":"If students offer air or water as a settled answer, record the idea as a possibility and ask what evidence would help test it.","source":"194","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"Q","label":"Slide Q","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-04-q.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 4 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":18}],"cue":{"do":"Save the question about where new plant matter came from.","ask":"Where might the extra matter have come from when the plants gained weight?","listen":"A possible source and a question the class can test next."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":33,"prep":34,"briefing":36,"resume":37,"teaching":38,"teachingEnd":39,"field":40,"checklist":35},"access":"During the data gallery, have partners point to the two compared values and name what stayed the same before writing a claim.","evidence":"Ask for the two plants or conditions being compared, the change each showed, and one reason the comparison can or cannot support the claim. If students compare final weights only, ask them to mark the starting values before returning to their claim.","checklist":["Queue Plant Growth Investigation and the class questions from Lesson 3.","Copy Investigation Setup, Interpret Plant Data, and Digging Into Soil for each student.","Sort Plant Data for small groups and prepare the station graphs. Keep results out of sight until needed.","Prepare Growing Ideas copies or student notebooks. Keep the teacher key separate."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1hBK9UHWO4vSWAA-EKnBBtUm7uPGoqwoZv6yVFtnRoXY/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 4 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-04.jpg"},{"number":5,"title":"Matter in the air","question":"Where does a plant get the stuff it grows from?","story":"The plant data gave us clues about conditions for growth. Today we’re asking where the added matter could come from. Let the scale and syringe give students evidence to argue with. Save the question about what light does for the next lesson.","science":"Use Lesson 4 weight comparisons to ask what supplies the extra matter. Air and water are material sources. Light provides energy, which Lesson 6 investigates. A scale reading alone does not prove what light is.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 05\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nWhere does a plant get the stuff it grows\nfrom?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 05\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nThe plant data gave us clues about conditions for growth. Today we’re asking where the added matter could\ncome from. Let the scale and syringe give students evidence to argue with. Save the question about what\nlight does for the next lesson.\nHere is the mystery. A pumpkin gets bigger and heavier. Where did all that extra stuff come from? In\nLesson 4, students compared plants grown under different conditions. Those comparisons gave them clues\nabout what helps plants grow. Today they ask a sharper question: Which of those things can supply the\nmatter that becomes part of a plant?\n1. Bring back the evidence | 5 min. Reopen the Lesson 4 plant data. Ask which plants gained weight and\nwhat students still wonder about where that extra matter came from.\n2. Put the candidates to the test | 20 min. Students compare weights with the scale. The basketball is the\nsurprise: ask what changed when they added air. Have groups record actual measurements with units. If the\nreadings barely change, compare measurements and check the setup before drawing a conclusion.\n3. Compare the results | 10 min. Pool the class data. Ask, “What result would convince you that air is made\nof matter?” Water adds measurable weight. Air can too. The flashlight comparison does not show added\nweight from light. Save the question of what light does for Lesson 6.\n4. Make the invisible visible | 25 min. Use the needleless syringe to explore how trapped air takes up\nspace and can be squeezed into a smaller space. Have students sketch particles with less space between\nthem when compressed. Ask where the particles went. If students say they vanished or got smaller, have\nthem compare their model with what the trapped air did.\n5. Go back to the pumpkin | 20 min. Have each student model air and water as sources of matter for\ngrowth. Ask for an arrow or label that shows where the matter comes from, plus one observation or result\nthat supports the idea.\n6. Leave a question for next time | 10 min. Return to the Driving Question Board. Plants need light, too. In\nLesson 6, students use solar toys to investigate what light can do and begin to explain how light supplies\nenergy for growth. Later they bring these ideas back to the nurse log model.\nAt the 45-minute bell. The publisher sequence puts you about 10 minutes into the syringe activity. Pause at\na safe point and save the class data chart and any particle sketches. Next time, reopen that work and finish\nthe model. If you already taught part of Lesson 5, resume from the last activity your class actually completed.\nSOURCE / OPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 TEACHER GUIDE, PRINTED PP. 203–207","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 05\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\nTest the shared digital scale. Confirm it can detect the difference after adding air to the same ball. Use a\npartially inflated, full-size sports ball that takes a needle, with the approved pump and needle. Follow the\nguide’s equipment directions. Use small plastic syringes for the trapped-air model. Do not substitute\nballoons or beach balls.\nGATHER BY ROLE\nFor about 22 students, stage roughly six group trays. Each needs an empty paper cup, a cup of water, a\nflashlight, and a partially inflated sports ball. Provide group access to the shared scale and the\nInvestigating Matter directions. Set out about 11 small plastic syringes for partners and colored pencils\nor markers for individual models. Prepare the Share Our Findings About Matter and How Can Air Inflate\na Basketball? charts. Have the Driving Question Board ready.\nPRINT AND REUSE\nOne Investigate Matter set per student (Student Bundle PDF 53–54) for C–D. One Model to Explain\nPlants & Matter set per student (PDF 55–56) for L–M. Plan for about 22 of each per class. Bring each\nstudent’s Interpret Plant Data (PDF 43–46) from Lesson 4 and have the shared results ready for A–B.\nReprint only missing copies.\nOPEN AND RESET\nOpen the official Lesson 5 deck and keep Teacher Guide printed pp. 206 and 208 at hand for setup and\nformative checks. Save the readings and unfinished models for each class, then reset the ball and tray\nsupplies before the next section.\nSource: OpenSciEd Teacher Guide printed pp. 206, 208 and 210–223. Printed p. 206 is PDF viewer p. 212.","resume":["Pause at a safe point in the syringe activity.","Class chart and any unfinished syringe models.","Resume the unfinished F–K activity. For current-year progress, confirm the class and last activity before resuming."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Reopen plant-weight patterns","ref":"Slides A–B","minutes":"5 min","move":"Reopen the Lesson 4 plant data. Ask which plants gained weight and what students still wonder about where that extra matter came from.","ask":"What made us think the plant gained matter, and what remains unexplained?","listen":"If students name what the plant needs, ask them to separate that list from the question of what added to its weight.","source":"209","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2},{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3}],"cue":{"do":"Reopen the Lesson 4 data and name one plant that gained weight.","ask":"Where might the added matter in that plant have come from?","listen":"A possible source linked to a measured gain."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides C–D · Compare readings on the scale."},{"title":"Compare readings on the scale","ref":"Slides C–D","minutes":"20 min","move":"At C, have partners propose how to weigh water, light, and air. At D, give each student Investigate Matter. In groups of three or four, record predictions, run the comparisons, and revisit predictions in Part C.","ask":"How could we add air to the same ball and measure the change? What should stay the same between readings?","listen":"If students say air cannot be weighed, ask how to trap it. Record the actual readings and units. Use a needle-inflated sports ball, following the guide’s equipment directions.","source":"210–211","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5}],"cue":{"do":"Have partners plan how to weigh air, water, and light. At D, run the handout comparisons and record readings with units.","ask":"How could we add air to the same ball and measure the change?","listen":"The same ball is compared before and after adding air."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides E · Pool the class readings."},{"title":"Pool the class readings","ref":"Slides E","minutes":"10 min","move":"Gather students with their handouts around Share Our Findings About Matter. Chart what groups measured separately from their claims.","ask":"Did every group see the same change? What did the scale show?","listen":"If air readings disagree or the change is too small to resolve, check the scale and setup before repeating. Keep the question of what light supplies open.","source":"212–214","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6}],"cue":{"do":"Chart the readings separately from what groups think they mean.","ask":"Did every group see the same change? Which readings show it?","listen":"A claim grounded in measured change, with disagreement or uncertainty acknowledged."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides F–K · Model trapped air."},{"title":"Model trapped air","ref":"Slides F–K","minutes":"25 min","move":"At G, show the plunger with the tip open. At H, have pairs or trios predict and try with the tip covered. Compare observations before revising particle sketches.","ask":"Why can the plunger move partway when air is trapped?","listen":"If students shrink or erase dots, ask where the trapped air could have gone. Compare sketches with the same number of particles. Save unfinished models and the data chart at the bell.","source":"215–220","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Show the open syringe at G. At H, have pairs predict and try with the tip covered.","ask":"Why can the plunger move partway when the air is trapped?","listen":"The same number of particles in less space, rather than particles disappearing."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides L–M · Model where plant matter comes from."},{"title":"Model where plant matter comes from","ref":"Slides L–M","minutes":"20 min","move":"Have each student label a source of matter and connect it to the plant on their model.","ask":"Which observation or measurement supports this arrow?","listen":"When students label light as added matter, return to the class chart. Carry the question of what light supplies to Lesson 6.","source":"221–223","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13},{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14}],"cue":{"do":"Have students label an arrow from a matter source to the plant.","ask":"Which observation or measurement supports this arrow?","listen":"A labeled connection from air or water to the plant, backed by evidence."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides N–O · Save the question about light."},{"title":"Save the question about light","ref":"Slides N–O","minutes":"10 min","move":"Return to the Driving Question Board. Plants need light, too. In Lesson 6, students use solar toys to investigate what light can do and begin to explain how light supplies energy for growth. Later they bring these ideas back to the nurse log model.","ask":"Which question about light is still open after our investigation?","listen":"If students conclude that light is unnecessary because it was not weighed, revisit the plant growth evidence and keep its role open for Lesson 6.","source":"224","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15},{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-05-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 5 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Return to the Driving Question Board and keep the role of light open.","ask":"What do we still need to figure out about light?","listen":"A question about what light does for growth."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":41,"prep":42,"briefing":44,"resume":45,"teaching":46,"teachingEnd":47,"field":48,"checklist":43},"access":"During the syringe model, offer dot sketches and oral rehearsal. Ask students to explain what changed and what stayed constant.","evidence":"Listen for a measurement supporting the air claim and look for air/water entering the plant model. If an arrow is unlabeled, ask the student to explain what moves and which evidence supports it.","checklist":["Test the scale and stage the approved pump, needle, and partially inflated sports ball.","Set up group trays with a paper cup, a cup of water, flashlight, and ball. Set out small plastic syringes for partners.","Copy Investigate Matter and Model to Explain Plants & Matter. Reuse Interpret Plant Data from Lesson 4.","Prepare the class data chart, air model chart, and Driving Question Board. Reset trays and the ball between sections."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/17b6fcJu0rCUv8O4R4BEYGUoZFxgu72OUkfHF1fKKeh8/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 5 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-05.jpg"},{"number":6,"title":"Powered by light","question":"Light makes things happen. What does it give a plant?","story":"Last time students built a case for air and water as sources of matter. Light still needs an explanation. Use the plant observations, solar toys, and reading to distinguish what light does from what air and water provide. At the close, bring that thinking back to the moss questions.","science":"Lesson 5 identified air and water as sources of matter. Sunlight supplies energy. Solar toys provide evidence that light can cause change; the reading supplies the evidence about how plants use that energy.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 06\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nLight makes things happen. What does it\ngive a plant?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 06\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nLast time students built a case for air and water as sources of matter. Light still needs an explanation.\nUse the plant observations, solar toys, and reading to distinguish what light does from what air and\nwater provide. At the close, bring that thinking back to the moss questions.\nHere is the mystery. Lesson 5 identified air and water as sources of matter for growth. Students now\ninvestigate what light contributes.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Discuss what we figured out in the last lesson about how plants get\ntheir matter from air and water, but we are unsure how sunlight helps plants to grow.\n2. Make observations | 10 min. Watch a video of plants to guide our predictions about how plants use\nlight to grow and discuss our initial ideas about how sunlight can cause changes.\n3. Carry out an investigation | 15 min. Observe how the Sun changes solar toys.\n4. Participate in a discussion | 15 min. Share our observations and discuss how the energy\ntransferred from sunlight causes changes.\n5. Read an article | 20 min. Read to obtain evidence about how plants transfer and use energy from\nthe Sun to grow.\n6. Develop a model to explain | 15 min. Individually, we model to explain that a plant (of our choice)\ntransfers sunlight for energy to grow, using air and water for matter.\n7. Update our Growing Ideas charts | 5 min. Individually update our Growing Ideas charts to\ndocument our new ideas about how plants use matter and energy to grow.\n8. Decide where to go next | 5 min. Summarize our ideas about plant growth and think about how\nwhat we have figured out about plants connects back to the nurse log.\nWhere it leads. In Lesson 7, students use their evidence about matter and energy to revise the\nnurse-log model.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 225–227\nHOST SEGMENT","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 06\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Test the solar toys in the planned sunlight or approved alternate setup. Open the plant-growth video\nand prepare the modeling handout.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Investigate Sunlight Lab • Investigate Sunlight • Observe Plants Growing • Tomatoes for Sale! Buy\nFresh Grown Tomatoes Today • Model to Explain Plants & Energy • My Growing Ideas\nPer small group:\n• Investigate Sunlight Lab • solar toy\nPer class:\n• Changing the Direction of Light During Plant Growth • Our Ideas About How Light Helps Plants Grow\nchart paper • What types of changes does sunlight cause? chart paper\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 228 (PDF viewer p. 234).\nBefore class\nG228 and 248. One tested solar toy per group of 3–4 (about six per section). Try the intended\nconditions before class. Use the publisher lamp alternative only with school procedures and appropriate\nequipment. Queue the plant and solar toy media, gather the prior models and set up the discussion\nchart. See Student Bundle PDF 57, 59, 61, 63 and confirm roles before copying.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nStudent copies\nOne Observe Plants Growing (57) for the plant video, Investigate Sunlight (59) for the solar toy, and\nModel to Explain Plants & Energy (61) for the model. Use My Growing Ideas (63) for reflection or\ncontinue in notebooks. About 22 sets per section.\nGroup and teacher prep\nOne tested solar toy and access to Investigate Sunlight Lab directions per group of 3–4. Queue the\ndirection-of-light media and Tomatoes for Sale reading from the publisher materials. Keep the teacher\ntranscripts and instructional guidance (guide pp. 249–251) handy.","resume":["The planned 45-minute boundary follows F–H.","Solar-toy observations and the discussion chart.","Open I–K. Ask what the reading can tell us about plants that the toys cannot."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Reopen the light question","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"5 min","move":"Discuss what we figured out in the last lesson about how plants get their matter from air and water, but we are unsure how sunlight helps plants to grow.","ask":"What did Lesson 5 help us explain about the plant’s matter, and what is still puzzling about light?","listen":"If students combine light, air, and water into one source, have them point to what their previous model actually explained.","source":"233","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Review the air and water evidence and bring back the open question about sunlight.","ask":"What did we figure out about matter, and what do we still wonder about light?","listen":"Air and water as matter sources, with the role of light still open."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B–D · Observe plants responding to light."},{"title":"Observe plants responding to light","ref":"Slides B–D","minutes":"10 min","move":"Use Observe Plants Growing to record what changes when light direction changes. Give students time to distinguish the visible change from their explanation.","ask":"What could we observe directly in the video?","listen":"Record claims about light entering as matter as ideas to test. Keep the observations for the next comparison.","source":"234–235","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5}],"cue":{"do":"Record what changes when light direction changes. Separate visible change from explanation.","ask":"What could we observe directly in the video?","listen":"A named change in growth or direction rather than a claim about the mechanism."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides E · Investigate the solar toy."},{"title":"Investigate the solar toy","ref":"Slides E","minutes":"15 min","move":"Give each group the solar toy and each student Investigate Sunlight. Compare movement under the assigned light conditions and record the result.","ask":"What changed when the light changed? What stayed the same?","listen":"If the toy does not respond, check the equipment and light condition before interpreting. Keep observations for F–H.","source":"236–240","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6}],"cue":{"do":"Give groups the solar toy and record movement under the assigned light conditions.","ask":"What changed when the light changed? What stayed the same?","listen":"A comparison tied to the light condition and the observed movement."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides F–H · Discuss light and change."},{"title":"Discuss light and change","ref":"Slides F–H","minutes":"15 min","move":"Build the class chart from observed changes. Use the toy’s movement and the plant’s change in direction to introduce the question the tomato reading will help answer.","ask":"What changed in each case, and what do we still need to explain about the plant?","listen":"If students say the toy proves how plants use light, ask which observation comes from the toy and what evidence we would need about the plant itself.","source":"237–241","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9}],"cue":{"do":"Pool the solar-toy observations before explaining how light can cause change.","ask":"Which observation shows the toy responded when the light changed?","listen":"A comparison from the trial, distinct from a claim about what plants do."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides I–K · Read how plants use sunlight."},{"title":"Read how plants use sunlight","ref":"Slides I–K","minutes":"20 min","move":"Reopen the toy observations. Use the tomato reading to connect energy transfer to plants and ask students to carry that evidence into L–M.","ask":"What can the reading tell us about plants that the toy could not?","listen":"If students label light as plant material, ask what supplies matter and what supplies energy. At O, return to the moss questions.","source":"242–248","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Use the tomato reading alongside the toy observations. Carry the evidence into modeling.","ask":"What can the reading tell us about plants that the toy could not?","listen":"Evidence distinguishing sunlight’s energy role from sources of matter."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides L–M · Model light, air, and water."},{"title":"Model light, air, and water","ref":"Slides L–M","minutes":"15 min","move":"Individually, we model to explain that a plant (of our choice) transfers sunlight for energy to grow, using air and water for matter.","ask":"Where does your model show matter entering, and how does it show energy from sunlight?","listen":"If all arrows mean the same thing, have students label what each arrow represents and connect one label to evidence from the reading.","source":"244–246","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13},{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14}],"cue":{"do":"Have students model a plant of their choice with labeled inputs.","ask":"Which arrow shows a source of matter, and which shows an energy source?","listen":"Air and water labeled as matter sources and light as an energy source."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides N · Update Growing Ideas."},{"title":"Update Growing Ideas","ref":"Slides N","minutes":"5 min","move":"Individually update our Growing Ideas charts to document our new ideas about how plants use matter and energy to grow.","ask":"Which idea in your model changed, and what evidence helped you revise it?","listen":"If the entry is just a list of plant needs, ask for one sentence or labeled sketch explaining the role of a specific input.","source":"247","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15}],"cue":{"do":"Have students record a new idea about how plants grow and the evidence behind it.","ask":"Which result or reading changed your thinking?","listen":"A supported distinction between matter and energy."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides O · Return to the nurse log."},{"title":"Return to the nurse log","ref":"Slides O","minutes":"5 min","move":"Reopen students’ models and Growing Ideas to review what plants need. Bring the class back to its moss questions for Lesson 7.","ask":"What might our plant evidence help us explain about moss on a nurse log?","listen":"If students treat the model as proof of every moss condition, have them name one supported connection and one question for the next lesson.","source":"248","id":8,"slides":[{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-06-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 6 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Review models and Growing Ideas. Reopen the class’s moss questions.","ask":"How could these ideas help us explain plants growing on the log?","listen":"A plant-growth idea students can apply or investigate in Lesson 7."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":49,"prep":50,"briefing":52,"resume":53,"teaching":54,"teachingEnd":55,"field":56,"checklist":51},"access":"After the solar-toy trial, offer a two-frame sketch of the same toy in two conditions. Ask students to label what changed and what light supplied.","evidence":"Look for an energy arrow from sunlight and separate matter arrows for air and water. If students label all arrows as matter, ask them to trace what becomes part of the plant and what supplies energy.","checklist":["Test each solar toy under the planned light conditions. Check the publisher alternative if needed.","Queue the plant observation media and the tomato reading.","Copy Observe Plants Growing, Investigate Sunlight, and Model to Explain Plants & Energy.","Set out the sunlight discussion charts and bring earlier class models."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1sohvNuDzOG8rkN7mVGWrx8nvRp2v-RFze84G7Q7BZoA/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 6 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-06.jpg"},{"number":7,"title":"Plants on the log","question":"Bring the evidence back to the log.","story":"We’ve gathered evidence about air, water, and light. Now ask students to put those ideas to work in a model of the nurse log. Their explanations tell us what they can account for about plants. Next we’ll shift our attention to the changing log and the animals that live there.","science":"Return to the class model with evidence from Lessons 3–6. A nurse log can provide conditions that support growth. Plants acquire material chiefly from air and water and use energy from light.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 07\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nBring the evidence back to the log.\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 07\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nWe’ve gathered evidence about air, water, and light. Now ask students to put those ideas to work in a\nmodel of the nurse log. Their explanations tell us what they can account for about plants. Next we’ll shift\nour attention to the changing log and the animals that live there.\nHere is the mystery. Students now have evidence about matter from air and water and energy from\nsunlight. Have them use those ideas to explain how plants live and grow on a nurse log.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Revisit what we have figured out so far about plants.\n2. Build a Gotta-Have-It Checklist | 10 min. Collaboratively build a Gotta-Have-It Checklist to identify\nwhat we need to include as we model our new ideas about plants.\n3. Develop a model to explain | 25 min. Individually and then model our new ideas about plants in the\nnurse log system.\n4. Connect to our communities | 10 min. With a trusted community member, we identify evidence of\nobjects or factors in our community that help plants grow.\n5. Argue from evidence | 30 min. Individually, we write a scientific argument to identify environments\nthat are best and least able to support plant growth.\n6. Revisit our Driving Question Board | 10 min. Revisit our DQB to see what questions we have\nanswered and determine where we should go next.\nWhere it leads. Save the revised models. Lesson 8 shifts attention to changes in the log and the\nanimals in the system.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 252–254","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 07\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Gather the earlier class models and charts, and prepare the checklist space. Separate the plant\nassessment and rubric from the regular handouts.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• My Growing Ideas charts and investigation handouts from prior lessons • Revised Nurse Log Model •\nWhat helps plants grow in our community? • Plants Growing in Different Places • Self-Evaluation Rubric\n• 1-2 sticky notes\nPer class:\n• charts and models from Lessons 3-6 • Gotta-Have-It Checklist chart paper • Class Consensus Model\nchart paper • Driving Question Board\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 255 (PDF viewer p. 261).\nBefore class\nG255; Student Bundle PDF 65–68, 135–136 and 143–146. Bring investigation records, earlier models\nand Growing Ideas. Prepare checklist/model space and 1–2 sticky notes per student. Separate the\nindividual assessment and rubric. If color copies are unavailable, the guide identifies slide H as an\nimage option.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nStudent practice copies\nOne Revised Nurse Log Model (65–66) per student for model revision. Bring earlier investigation pages\nand Growing Ideas. Copy the community page (135–136) per student if assigning it.\nHold for assessment\nOne Plants Growing in Different Places (143–146) and one Self-Evaluation Rubric (67–68) per student.\nDistribute the task at G and use the self-check when directed. Slide H can display the photos if color\ncopies are unavailable. Keep the scoring guide (printed pp. 275 onward) teacher-only.","resume":["Aim to finish the model before pausing.","Checklist, model revisions, and any notes from F.","Resume F if unfinished. Protect the G–I argument and assessment window."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Revisit the plant findings","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"5 min","move":"Revisit what we have figured out so far about plants.","ask":"Which earlier observation gives us evidence about what plants need?","listen":"If students list air, water, or light from memory, ask them to point to the investigation, model, or reading behind one idea.","source":"260","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Reopen student records about what plants need and where their matter comes from.","ask":"Which finding should our nurse-log model account for?","listen":"A result from an earlier plant investigation."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B–C · Build the modeling checklist."},{"title":"Build the modeling checklist","ref":"Slides B–C","minutes":"10 min","move":"Reopen investigation records and Growing Ideas. Build the checklist from findings students can support before they revise models.","ask":"Which observation or comparison belongs behind this checklist idea?","listen":"If a claim is general, have students point to one artifact and name the finding it shows. Keep unresolved ideas visible.","source":"262–264","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4}],"cue":{"do":"Build the class checklist from findings students can support.","ask":"Which observation or comparison belongs behind this checklist idea?","listen":"A specific artifact and the finding it shows."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides D–E · Revise nurse-log models."},{"title":"Revise nurse-log models","ref":"Slides D–E","minutes":"25 min","move":"Give students time to revise individual models. Use selected examples in the consensus discussion and ask students to trace their arrows.","ask":"Which part shows matter entering a plant? Which shows the source of energy?","listen":"If an arrow is unlabeled, have its author trace it aloud and add a label. Save a revision to revisit.","source":"265–268","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6}],"cue":{"do":"Give students time to revise individually, then compare examples in the consensus discussion.","ask":"Which arrow shows matter entering a plant? Which shows the energy source?","listen":"A labeled relationship connected to investigation evidence."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides F · Connect to community evidence."},{"title":"Connect to community evidence","ref":"Slides F","minutes":"10 min","move":"With a trusted community member, we identify evidence of objects or factors in our community that help plants grow.","ask":"What could you observe in your community that would help explain how a plant grows there?","listen":"If students name a place only, ask them to identify one observable condition and explain why it might matter.","source":"270","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7}],"cue":{"do":"Invite students to look for local examples of conditions that help plants grow.","ask":"Which observation from your community connects to a condition we investigated?","listen":"A concrete example linked to a plant-growth condition."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides G–I · Administer the plant transfer task."},{"title":"Administer the plant transfer task","ref":"Slides G–I","minutes":"30 min","move":"Distribute Plants Growing in Different Places at G. Preserve individual argument writing and use the publisher self-evaluation rubric when directed.","ask":"Clarify directions within the publisher guidance and save instructional prompts for afterward.","listen":"Use the scoring tool to identify missing evidence connections. Follow up after collecting the individual work.","source":"271–272","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10}],"cue":{"do":"Distribute Plants Growing in Different Places at G. Protect individual argument writing and use the rubric as directed.","ask":"Invite clarifying questions about the directions within the publisher’s assessment guidance.","listen":"After collection, check whether a chosen condition is connected to evidence."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides J–K · Revisit the question board."},{"title":"Revisit the question board","ref":"Slides J–K","minutes":"10 min","move":"Revisit our DQB to see what questions we have answered and determine where we should go next.","ask":"Which question can we answer with evidence now, and which still needs investigation?","listen":"If students want to remove a question because the topic came up, ask them to match it with a finding or keep it open.","source":"273–274","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-07-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 7 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Sort questions the plant evidence helped answer and those still open.","ask":"Which question can we answer now, and what is still unresolved?","listen":"A question linked to evidence or marked as needing more evidence."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":57,"prep":58,"briefing":60,"resume":61,"teaching":62,"teachingEnd":63,"field":64,"checklist":59},"access":"During model practice before the transfer task, have partners point to an arrow and rehearse which evidence supports it.","evidence":"Have a student explain one specific change from the earlier model and the observation behind it. If the explanation lacks evidence, prompt a return to the relevant investigation record before the independent task.","checklist":["Gather earlier investigation pages, Growing Ideas, and the class models.","Prepare the class checklist, consensus model space, and sticky notes.","Copy Revised Nurse Log Model and any assigned community page.","Hold the transfer task and student self-evaluation rubric for the appropriate step. Keep teacher scoring separate."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1kowGGXn6osOmYAcIFfF32QzKYMHkj4dTrMUYl4dHJf0/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 7 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-07.jpg"},{"number":8,"title":"The ghost log","question":"The log looks smaller. Where did its matter go?","story":"So far the log has been a place where plants grow. Now look at the log itself: it seems to shrink. Students compare observations and termite data to look for where the matter might go. That sets up the next question about what animals get and use from food.","science":"Lesson 7 explained plant growth. Now the log looks smaller over time. Appearance can start a question; termite colony evidence is needed to reason about where matter might move.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 08\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nThe log looks smaller. Where did its matter\ngo?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 08\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nSo far the log has been a place where plants grow. Now look at the log itself: it seems to shrink.\nStudents compare observations and termite data to look for where the matter might go. That sets up the\nnext question about what animals get and use from food.\nHere is the mystery. The class has modeled plant growth on the log. Now they investigate why a log\nseems to disappear and what might happen to its matter.\n1. Take stock of our questions | 5 min. Consider what other questions we had about nurse logs,\nespecially how nurse logs change over time.\n2. Make observations | 15 min. With the class divided into two halves, we observe the Nurse Log in a\nBox and a series of images showing nurse logs gradually seeming to disappear.\n3. Ask questions | 10 min. Generate new claims and testable questions about the unexplained\nphenomenon of ghost logs.\n4. Read a book | 15 min. Read a book about how nurse logs help animals meet their needs for survival,\nwith a focus on those animals that eat the log itself.\n5. Make sense of data | 20 min. Analyze data from a closed system containing termites and a nurse\nlog.\n6. Participate in a discussion | 20 min. Share our findings in a discussion to answer the lesson\nquestion, “Why does a nurse log seem to lose matter over time?”.\n7. Decide where to go next | 5 min. Individually, we begin to consider what else animals do with the\nfood that they eat, other than gain matter.\nWhere it leads. The termite evidence sets up Lesson 9’s question about what animals do with the\nenergy they get from food.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 278–280","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 08\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Check the nurse-log terrarium and hand lenses. Pull the earlier moss questions and queue the\nnurse-log book and termite colony data.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Nurse Logs Over Time • Analyze Data of a Termite Colony\nPer partners:\n• Weight of a Termite Colony in a Closed System\nPer small group:\n• hand lenses\nPer class:\n• Driving Question Board • Nurse Log in a Box • Notice and Wonder chart • Our Questions About Moss\nchart (from Lesson 2) • Testable Questions and Evidence to Collect chart paper (optional) • Nurse Logs:\nMore than Meets the Eye • Extension questions for termite data • Analyze Termite Data: Teacher Key\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 281 (PDF viewer p. 287).\nBefore class\nG281. Check the Nurse Log in a Box and stage lenses per guide. Pull DQB and earlier moss questions.\nHave the nurse-log book or projection ready, plus termite data and student recording pages (Student\nBundle viewer 69–77, 79). Sort partner data from individual recording pages.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nStudent copies\nOne Nurse Logs Over Time (69–72) for observation stations and Analyze Data of a Termite Colony\n(73–77) for O. Copy My Growing Ideas (79) for reflection or use notebooks.\nPair and teacher copies\nOne Weight of a Termite Colony in a Closed System graph per pair or small group, from the publisher\nresource, to accompany O. Keep Analyze Termite Data: Teacher Key (printed pp. 307–308) and\noptional extension (pp. 309–310) separate.","resume":["Finish M before stopping.","Observations, book evidence, and competing questions.","Begin N–O with the first data comparison. Termite data notes are produced in Block 2."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Take stock of log questions","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"5 min","move":"Consider what other questions we had about nurse logs, especially how nurse logs change over time.","ask":"Which question would help explain why the log looks different over time?","listen":"If students move directly to a cause, return to what has changed and use their idea to choose what to investigate.","source":"285","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Choose a class question about how the nurse log changes over time.","ask":"What change could we look for in different observations of the log?","listen":"An observable difference that might help answer the question."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B–I · Observe changes at stations."},{"title":"Observe changes at stations","ref":"Slides B–I","minutes":"15 min","move":"Have students record observations by station on Nurse Logs Over Time. Label the source and separate observed change from explanations.","ask":"Which source shows a change? What could tell us where the matter went?","listen":"If students say matter disappeared, put that idea on the question list for comparison with the book and colony data.","source":"287–290","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10}],"cue":{"do":"Use Nurse Logs Over Time. Have students label each observation by station source.","ask":"Which source shows a change in the log?","listen":"A recorded change separated from a possible cause."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides J–L · Ask testable ghost-log questions."},{"title":"Ask testable ghost-log questions","ref":"Slides J–L","minutes":"10 min","move":"Generate new claims and testable questions about the unexplained phenomenon of ghost logs.","ask":"What evidence would let us distinguish your explanation from another one?","listen":"If students propose that the log simply disappeared, help them name a possible process and an observation or measurement that could test it.","source":"291–293","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13}],"cue":{"do":"Record competing claims about the ghost log and turn wonderings into testable questions.","ask":"What could we observe or measure to tell where the matter went?","listen":"A possible test or data comparison, rather than a conclusion."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides M · Read about animals on the log."},{"title":"Read about animals on the log","ref":"Slides M","minutes":"15 min","move":"Read Nurse Logs: More than Meets the Eye aloud. Distinguish animals that use the log for shelter from animals that eat the wood.","ask":"Which animal does the book identify as eating the wood? What might we measure to test where some of that matter goes?","listen":"If students say termites explain the entire loss, record it as a claim to test. Ask what weight data would help distinguish eating some wood from accounting for all the missing matter.","source":"294–295","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14}],"cue":{"do":"Read the publisher book and track evidence about animals that eat material from the log.","ask":"What does the book say an animal gets from the log?","listen":"A specific detail about a resource or feeding relationship."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides N–O · Interpret termite-colony data."},{"title":"Interpret termite-colony data","ref":"Slides N–O","minutes":"20 min","move":"At O, give each student the analysis handout and share the colony graph by pair or group. Model locating one component and reading the units.","ask":"What happened to the weight of one component between these readings?","listen":"If students read across unlike components, trace one row over time. Record two values before interpreting.","source":"296–299","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15},{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"At O, share the colony graph and analysis handout. Model locating one component and its units.","ask":"What happened to the weight of this component between two readings?","listen":"Two values for the same component and a correctly described trend."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides P–T · Discuss where the matter goes."},{"title":"Discuss where the matter goes","ref":"Slides P–T","minutes":"20 min","move":"Compare changes in the log and other components. Let students label a possible destination for matter and identify which part is an inference.","ask":"What did we measure? What destination does the evidence suggest?","listen":"If students expect one gain to equal the entire log loss, name the measured parts and what the data cannot track.","source":"300–305","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17},{"id":"Q","label":"Slide Q","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-q.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":18},{"id":"R","label":"Slide R","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-r.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":19},{"id":"S","label":"Slide S","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-s.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":20},{"id":"T","label":"Slide T","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-t.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":21}],"cue":{"do":"Compare trends in the log and other components. Label possible destinations separately from measured values.","ask":"What did we measure, and what destination does it suggest?","listen":"A proposed matter pathway with the dataset’s limits acknowledged."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides U · Ask what food does for animals."},{"title":"Ask what food does for animals","ref":"Slides U","minutes":"5 min","move":"Individually, we begin to consider what else animals do with the food that they eat, other than gain matter.","ask":"What do you think an animal does with the food it gets?","listen":"If students say only “it gets energy,” ask them to suggest something the animal could do using that energy and leave the claim open for investigation.","source":"306","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"U","label":"Slide U","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-08-u.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 8 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":22}],"cue":{"do":"Have students propose another role for the food an animal eats.","ask":"Besides gaining matter, what might an animal use its food for?","listen":"An idea or question about uses of food to investigate next."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":65,"prep":66,"briefing":68,"resume":69,"teaching":70,"teachingEnd":71,"field":72,"checklist":67},"access":"During the termite-data discussion, let students trace a row with a finger or ruler, then say the trend with two readings before writing.","evidence":"Ask students to connect one data pattern with a possible transfer of matter. If they use a photo as proof of a destination, return to the measurements and separate the two evidence sources.","checklist":["Inspect Nurse Log in a Box and stage hand lenses and observation stations.","Have the nurse-log reading and earlier moss questions ready.","Copy Nurse Logs Over Time and Analyze Data of a Termite Colony for each student.","Prepare colony graphs by pair or small group and keep the teacher key separate."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1_-TDGyUNRWvpImdVwAXwV7Ag95bGFWov818Wvd9YFUw/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 8 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-08.jpg"},{"number":9,"title":"Isopods on the move","question":"What do animals do with energy from food?","story":"The termite evidence helped us think about where matter goes when an animal eats. Today the isopod investigation and bird readings bring energy into the story. Have students keep what they observed separate from what they infer. They’ll use both kinds of evidence to explain animal survival next time.","science":"Lesson 8 traced matter from wood to termites. Here students investigate uses of energy and later trace it through food back to the Sun. Movement and temperature observations do not directly measure energy from food.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 09\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nWhat do animals do with energy from food?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 09\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nThe termite evidence helped us think about where matter goes when an animal eats. Today the isopod\ninvestigation and bird readings bring energy into the story. Have students keep what they observed\nseparate from what they infer. They’ll use both kinds of evidence to explain animal survival next time.\nHere is the mystery. Lesson 8 traced matter from wood to termites. Today students compare\nobservations of animal behavior and ask how energy from food helps animals live.\n1. Recall where we left off | 10 min. Revisit and share our ideas about what animals do with the food\nthey eat.\n2. Plan to explore | 10 min. Share what we know about pillbugs and isopods and learn more key\ndetails about this group of animals.\n3. Carry out an investigation | 15 min. Investigate how isopods use energy when heat and light are\nintroduced into their habitat.\n4. Participate in a discussion | 10 min. Interpret our data from the isopod investigation and connect it\nto the nurse log habitat.\n5. Read an article | 20 min. In pairs, we read and share about how birds keep warm in the winter to\nhighlight other ways that some animals use energy to balance their body temperature.\n6. Participate in a discussion | 20 min. Use what we have learned about how isopods and birds get\nand use energy to figure out what we can now say about animals in general.\n7. Take stock of what we figured out | 5 min. Review our new understanding to determine what we\ncan explain about the nurse log in the next lesson.\nWhere it leads. Keep the animal evidence for Lesson 10, when students use it to explain how the\nnurse-log system helps animals.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 314–316","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 09\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Set up the isopod terrarium, lamp and thermometers using the publisher reference. Check school\nhandling and heat-lamp procedures before class.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Investigate Isopods Lab • Investigate Isopods handout • Investigate Isopods • Feathered Friends •\nFeathered Friends Evidence Table\nPer class:\n• Investigate Isopods Lab • terrarium with isopods • terrarium lid or plastic tub • heat lamp • two\nthermometers • print out or re-creation of the Temperature for Isopod Investigation reference •\nstopwatch or other timekeeping device\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 317 (PDF viewer p. 323).\nBefore class\nG317–319, 338 and following setup pages; Student Bundle PDF 81–83, 85–92. Allow 60–90 minutes\nprep. Default to the publisher demonstration: isopod terrarium, clear lid or tub, heat lamp, two\nthermometers, temperature reference and timer. Follow care, school and heat-lamp procedures; reset\nand check the setup for section two. Publisher video is the approved alternative if the live setup is\nunavailable. Separate lab pages and bird reading/evidence pages; prepare individual copies as\ndirected.\nPrint per student\nInvestigate Isopods, Student Bundle PDF 81–83, before data collection. For N–O, give every student\none assigned bird article (PDF 85–86, 87–88, or 89–90) and one Evidence Table (PDF 91–92). Split\nabout 22 article copies among the three birds, based on reading pairs and mixed groups.\nShared demonstration\nPrint or recreate one Warm/Cool temperature reference for the demonstration (Teacher Guide printed p.\n340). Reuse it for both classes. Confirm the video alternative before class if the live demo is\nunavailable.\nTeacher only\nKeep the live setup and care reference (printed pp. 338–339) and formative guidance (p. 319) at the\ndemo station. Save class data before handing out the next block's readings.","resume":["Finish or safely close the live demonstration before the bell.","Demonstration data and the first discussion claims.","Start N–O with one unresolved question about animal energy."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Revisit animals and food","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"10 min","move":"Revisit and share our ideas about what animals do with the food they eat.","ask":"What did we find out about what enters an animal when it eats?","listen":"If students recall only the animal’s name, reopen the colony evidence and ask which changes gave them an idea about food.","source":"320","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Reopen students’ ideas about what animals do with their food.","ask":"What did the termite evidence help us explain about food?","listen":"A connection to the earlier observations or colony data."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B–F · Plan what to observe."},{"title":"Plan what to observe","ref":"Slides B–F","minutes":"10 min","move":"Use students’ sightings to introduce pillbugs and sowbugs as isopods. At F, connect the conditions under the nurse log to an isopod’s needs.","ask":"What would you expect to find under the log that would help an isopod survive?","listen":"If students say “they hide there,” ask them to name one feature of that habitat and explain how it helps. Shade, moisture, and decaying leaves offer different explanations to consider.","source":"321–322","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7}],"cue":{"do":"Elicit what students know about isopods and introduce the relevant habitat information.","ask":"What could we record if the isopods respond to a change in their surroundings?","listen":"A proposed observable behavior or measurement."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides G–J · Run the isopod investigation."},{"title":"Run the isopod investigation","ref":"Slides G–J","minutes":"15 min","move":"Take contrasting predictions before turning on the lamp or showing the video. Record starting temperatures, then count isopods and note temperatures on both sides at agreed intervals. Pause the video at evidence points if using it.","ask":"If they move toward the cooler side, how would the counts change over time?","listen":"Some students may predict that isopods move toward the light, as plants did. Ask them to connect their prediction to isopod habitat evidence and name the count pattern that would support it. Follow publisher and school guidance to close the live setup.","source":"323–327","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11}],"cue":{"do":"Elicit predictions before the demonstration or publisher video. Record temperature and movement separately.","ask":"What change in movement would let us compare the predictions?","listen":"A prediction that can be compared with a recorded observation."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides K–M · Interpret temperature and movement."},{"title":"Interpret temperature and movement","ref":"Slides K–M","minutes":"10 min","move":"Gather readings and behavior observations before explaining the response. Compare the warm condition to the publisher’s habitat reference.","ask":"What changed in the setting, and what did the animals do?","listen":"If students say the lamp gave food energy, separate external conditions from energy obtained from food. Save an unresolved question for the bird comparison.","source":"328–329","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13},{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14}],"cue":{"do":"Gather readings and observations before interpreting the response. Compare the warm condition to the habitat reference.","ask":"What changed in the setting, and what did the animals do?","listen":"A reading and a movement observation, rather than a claim that the lamp supplied food energy."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides N–O · Read the bird evidence."},{"title":"Read the bird evidence","ref":"Slides N–O","minutes":"20 min","move":"Give each student one assigned bird reading. Have students record evidence and share findings in mixed groups before the class comparison.","ask":"How does this bird use energy? Which detail supports the idea?","listen":"If students generalize from one bird, have them compare another reading before synthesizing.","source":"330–332","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15},{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Assign one bird reading per student. Record evidence and share across sources.","ask":"How does this bird use energy, and which detail supports your idea?","listen":"A claim about a specific bird supported by the reading."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides P–R · Compare birds and isopods."},{"title":"Compare birds and isopods","ref":"Slides P–R","minutes":"20 min","move":"Use the comparison chart to separate how each animal gets energy from how it uses energy to maintain body temperature.","ask":"What does the bird reading tell us that our isopod counts could not?","listen":"If students say both animals keep warm by moving to another place, return to the bird reading. Compare the isopod’s movement to a different location with the bird’s use of food energy to regulate temperature internally.","source":"333–334","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17},{"id":"Q","label":"Slide Q","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-q.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":18},{"id":"R","label":"Slide R","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-r.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":19}],"cue":{"do":"Compare the bird readings with the demonstration record before generalizing.","ask":"How are the ways these animals respond to temperature alike or different?","listen":"A comparison grounded in both sources."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides S · Take stock of animal evidence."},{"title":"Take stock of animal evidence","ref":"Slides S","minutes":"5 min","move":"Review our new understanding to determine what we can explain about the nurse log in the next lesson.","ask":"Which connection would you add to our nurse-log model using evidence from today?","listen":"If students give a general claim about animals, ask them to name an organism, an energy use, and the observation or reading that supports it.","source":"337","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"S","label":"Slide S","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-09-s.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 9 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":20}],"cue":{"do":"Record one explanation the class can use in the nurse-log model and one open question.","ask":"Which evidence should we carry into our model of animal survival?","listen":"A claim paired with a specific observation or reading."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":73,"prep":74,"briefing":76,"resume":77,"teaching":78,"teachingEnd":79,"field":80,"checklist":75},"access":"After the demonstration, let partners point to a thermometer reading or recorded observation and rehearse a claim that distinguishes temperature from movement.","evidence":"At K–M, look for a specific observation or reading supporting the claim. At P–R, ask students to trace food energy back through plants to sunlight. If the chain breaks, have them point to a relevant part of the reading or comparison chart.","checklist":["Set up the isopod demonstration with the terrarium, heat lamp, two thermometers, reference, and timer. Check school procedures.","Confirm the publisher video option if the live setup is unavailable.","Copy Investigate Isopods, one assigned bird reading per student, and the evidence table.","Stage the class data record and plan safe closure and reset between sections."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1DKrdLg5eRv3KBGbIHQXphRSvMIYs_eUffuBJoY_zut8/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 9 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-09.jpg"},{"number":10,"title":"Life around the log","question":"Can your model explain how an animal survives here?","story":"We’ve followed what animals get from food and how they use it. Today students bring that evidence back to the nurse-log model, then apply their reasoning to a new animal. Keep their explanations visible so the termite investigation can pick up the question of how animals use their food.","science":"Use the termite and isopod/bird evidence to link an animal's needs to parts of the nurse-log system. Use the official salamander task to assess transfer separately.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 10\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nCan your model explain how an animal\nsurvives here?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 10\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nWe’ve followed what animals get from food and how they use it. Today students bring that evidence\nback to the nurse-log model, then apply their reasoning to a new animal. Keep their explanations visible\nso the termite investigation can pick up the question of how animals use their food.\nHere is the mystery. Students bring together their evidence about how animals get and use matter and\nenergy. The model should show how the animal’s needs connect to specific parts of the system.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Navigate from the observations and questions we shared in the last\nlesson to today’s work of putting the pieces together for animals in the nurse log system.\n2. Build a Gotta-Have-It Checklist | 15 min. In small groups and then collaboratively build a\nGotta-Have-It Checklist.\n3. Develop a model to explain | 20 min. Individually, we model to explain our ideas about how nurse\nlogs help animals live and grow, then provide feedback to another student on their model.\n4. Participate in a discussion | 20 min. Participate in a Consensus Discussion as we model to explain\nwhat we agree on and what we still have questions on to explain how nurse logs help animals live and\ngrow.\n5. Connect to our communities | 5 min. Outside of class, we look for evidence of animals getting\nand/or using matter and energy in our community.\n6. Apply ideas to another phenomenon | 20 min. Individually, we complete an assessment in which\nwe develop and use a model that describes how animals use matter and energy from food.\n7. Revisit our Driving Question Board | 5 min. Revisit our Driving Question Board to see what\nquestions we have answered and determine where we should go next.\nWhere it leads. The salamander task gives students a chance to transfer what they learned. Lesson 11\nfollows a new question about how termites can use wood as food.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 344–346","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 10\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Gather the animal evidence and the checklist. Set aside the salamander transfer task and its teacher\nscoring guide for the assessment stop.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• My Growing Ideas charts from Lesson 8 (and optionally added into Lesson 9) • handouts from previous\ninvestigations • Revised Nurse Log Model • Animals in Our Community • Ensatina Salamanders &\nNurse Logs • 1-2 sticky notes\nPer partners:\n• Revised Nurse Log Model\nPer small group:\n• Lesson 10 Gotta-Have-It Checklist\nPer class:\n• Driving Question Board (optional) • Class Gotta-Have-It Checklist from Lesson 7 • charts and evidence\ntables from Lessons 8 and 9 • Nurse Log Consensus Model chart •\nDQB\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 347 (PDF viewer p. 353).\nBefore class\nG347; Student Bundle PDF 93–96, 137–138 and assessment 147–150. Bring models, Growing Ideas\nand earlier evidence tables. Prepare class checklist and 1–2 sticky notes/student. Print assessment\nsets separately. Use the publisher scoring and permitted-support guidance; keep it teacher-only.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nStudent and group practice\nOne Revised Nurse Log Model (95–96) per student. Partners review their individual models. Prepare\none Gotta-Have-It Checklist (93–94) per small group for the opening Explore. Copy Animals in Our\nCommunity (137–138) per student if assigning it.\nHold for assessment\nOne Ensatina Salamanders & Nurse Logs (147–150) per student for slide I. Keep it separate from\npractice and follow the official directions for administration. Keep the scoring guide (printed pp. 364\nonward) teacher-only.","resume":["If F cannot start and finish productively, pause after D–E.","Models and feedback.","Compare one pair of ideas. Protect the assessment window, allowing extra time if needed."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Reopen animal evidence","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"5 min","move":"Navigate from the observations and questions we shared in the last lesson to today’s work of putting the pieces together for animals in the nurse log system.","ask":"Which animal need can we now explain using evidence from a previous lesson?","listen":"If students only list animals, ask what one animal gets from its surroundings and point back to a source.","source":"350","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Bring back the prior evidence about what animals get and use from the nurse log.","ask":"Which observation helps explain how an animal survives there?","listen":"A link between an animal’s need and a named resource."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B–C · Build the animal checklist."},{"title":"Build the animal checklist","ref":"Slides B–C","minutes":"15 min","move":"Use prior models, charts, and evidence tables to build the animal checklist in groups before students draw.","ask":"What does the animal get from its surroundings? What evidence shows that?","listen":"If students list habitat features alone, ask which need each feature meets.","source":"351–353","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4}],"cue":{"do":"Use charts, models, and evidence tables to build the checklist before students draw.","ask":"What does the animal get from its surroundings, and what evidence shows that?","listen":"A resource, the need it meets, and a supporting source."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides D–E · Model and give feedback."},{"title":"Model and give feedback","ref":"Slides D–E","minutes":"20 min","move":"Have students draft individually. Partners trace a connection and give evidence-based feedback before revision.","ask":"Where does this arrow start and end? Which observation supports it?","listen":"If the arrow only links two organisms, ask the student to label what moves or what resource is provided.","source":"355–356","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6}],"cue":{"do":"Have students draft individually. Partners trace one connection and give evidence-based feedback.","ask":"Where does this arrow start and end, and which observation supports it?","listen":"A labeled transfer or resource connection supported by evidence."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides F · Discuss models and reach consensus."},{"title":"Discuss models and reach consensus","ref":"Slides F","minutes":"20 min","move":"Bring revised models to the Scientists Circle. Compare how the models represent a feeding connection before adding it to the class model.","ask":"Where does matter move when the animal eats? How can we show where the energy originally came from?","listen":"If students draw one unlabeled arrow, ask them to trace the feeding path aloud. Have the class agree on labels or symbols that distinguish matter movement and energy transfer.","source":"357–359","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7}],"cue":{"do":"Compare individual models and revise the class model where the evidence supports agreement.","ask":"Which connections can we agree on, and which do we still need to investigate?","listen":"A supported model relationship or a clearly marked uncertainty."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides G · Connect to local animal examples."},{"title":"Connect to local animal examples","ref":"Slides G","minutes":"5 min","move":"Outside of class, we look for evidence of animals getting and/or using matter and energy in our community.","ask":"What could you observe that would tell you how a local animal meets one need?","listen":"If students infer a diet from where they see an animal, have them separate the location they observed from the feeding evidence they would look for.","source":"360","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8}],"cue":{"do":"Invite students to look for an example of animals getting or using matter or energy in their community.","ask":"What could you observe that would support your example?","listen":"A concrete animal behavior or resource students can look for."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides H–I · Administer the salamander task."},{"title":"Administer the salamander task","ref":"Slides H–I","minutes":"20 min","move":"Move from class modeling to the new animal. Distribute Ensatina Salamanders & Nurse Logs at I and follow the publisher’s independent assessment directions.","ask":"Review each question, invite clarifying questions, and remind students which earlier evidence is available as the guide directs.","listen":"Score the relationship and evidence using the teacher tool. Follow up on incomplete connections after collection.","source":"361–362","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10}],"cue":{"do":"Distribute the official task at I. Review directions and follow the guide’s independent assessment conditions.","ask":"Invite clarifying questions about the task within the publisher’s guidance.","listen":"After collection, check the resource-to-survival relationship and its evidence."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides J–K · Revisit remaining questions."},{"title":"Revisit remaining questions","ref":"Slides J–K","minutes":"5 min","move":"Revisit our Driving Question Board to see what questions we have answered and determine where we should go next.","ask":"Which remaining question can the class now connect to evidence about animals?","listen":"If students suggest crossing out a question without explaining it, ask them to name the class observation, reading, or model that addresses it.","source":"363","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11},{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-10-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 10 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Return to the Driving Question Board and save questions about animals and food.","ask":"Which question about how animals use food remains open?","listen":"A question that can motivate the termite investigation."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":81,"prep":82,"briefing":84,"resume":85,"teaching":86,"teachingEnd":87,"field":88,"checklist":83},"access":"During consensus-model practice before the assessment, let students trace a path on the class model and name the evidence for one connection.","evidence":"Review one model connection before transfer and use the official scoring tool afterward. If the chain lacks a source, point students back to the relevant prior evidence during practice. Preserve the publisher's assessment conditions during H–I.","checklist":["Gather models, Growing Ideas, and evidence from Lessons 8 and 9.","Prepare the Gotta-Have-It Checklist, class model space, and sticky notes.","Copy individual Revised Nurse Log Model pages and the checklist for groups.","Hold the Ensatina Salamanders & Nurse Logs assessment for slide I and keep scoring guidance teacher-only."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1LKfRWdz-vQDPZIOSSvRuv49_iHMRfapJqgXnEs3GQcU/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 10 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-10.jpg"},{"number":11,"title":"Termites at work","question":"How can a termite make a meal out of wood?","story":"We’ve been explaining what animals need to survive. Termites bring us back to a puzzling example: wood is their food. Put the video and reading side by side so students can build an explanation from both. Then ask how another organism on the log, a mushroom, might get what it needs.","science":"Build on animals getting matter and energy from food. The video shows termites eating wood. The article supplies evidence about the role of organisms in their gut; a surface video cannot show that mechanism.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 11\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nHow can a termite make a meal out of\nwood?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 11\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nWe’ve been explaining what animals need to survive. Termites bring us back to a puzzling example:\nwood is their food. Put the video and reading side by side so students can build an explanation from\nboth. Then ask how another organism on the log, a mushroom, might get what it needs.\nHere is the mystery. Students already know animals use food for matter and energy. Wood makes this\nquestion interesting. Have them use the video and article to figure out what happens inside the termite.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Discuss what we have figured out about animals, matter, and\nenergy, which makes us wonder how termites can get matter and energy from wood in the nurse log.\n2. Make observations | 20 min. Watch a video of a termite eating wood, and then discuss our initial\nideas about how the termites use wood as a source of matter and energy.\n3. Read an article | 25 min. In partners, we read an article about what happens when termites eat\nwood to gather evidence to explain how termites get matter and energy from wood.\n4. Develop a model | 35 min. Model our ideas based on evidence to explain how termites get matter\nand energy from wood in the nurse log.\n5. Decide where to go next | 5 min. Discuss if other organisms in the nurse log system are able to\nbreak down the wood of the nurse log.\nWhere it leads. Save the explanation about microscopic organisms. In Lesson 12, students turn to\nother organisms that grow on the log.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 368–370","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 11\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Queue the termite video and prepare Termite Guts for partners. Stage the evidence organizer for the\ncomparison of the video and reading.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Termite Eating Wood handout • Evidence for Termite Matter and Energy handout\nPer partners:\n• Termite Guts handout\nPer class:\n• Termites Eating Wood video • optional Microscopic View of Termite Gut video\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 371 (PDF viewer p. 377).\nBefore class\nG371. Queue Termites Eating Wood and the optional microscopic video, verify access, prepare Termite\nGuts for partners and the evidence organizer. Student Bundle PDF 97–101. Use the guide's notebook\noption where appropriate.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nStudent and partner copies\nOne Termite Eating Wood (101) and Evidence for Termite Matter and Energy (99–100) per student.\nGive the observation sheet with the video and use the organizer across both evidence sources. Prepare\none Termite Guts reading (97–98) per pair for the reading activity.\nTeacher prep\nQueue Termites Eating Wood and the optional microscopic video. Use a notebook in place of a handout\nonly where the guide permits.","resume":["If stopping during F–H, note the paragraph reached.","Each pair’s evidence table.","Begin with a partner recap before finishing the reading."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Reopen animal-food evidence","ref":"Slides A–B","minutes":"5 min","move":"Discuss what we have figured out about animals, matter, and energy, which makes us wonder how termites can get matter and energy from wood in the nurse log.","ask":"What remains unexplained about getting matter and energy from wood?","listen":"If students say the video of eating will answer everything, ask what it could show from outside and what evidence they would need about the inside.","source":"374","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2},{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3}],"cue":{"do":"Revisit what the class knows about animals, matter, and energy before introducing the termite puzzle.","ask":"How could wood provide matter and energy to a termite?","listen":"An initial idea or question about what happens to the wood."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides C–E · Observe termites eating wood."},{"title":"Observe termites eating wood","ref":"Slides C–E","minutes":"20 min","move":"Have students watch Termites Eating Wood and record observations and questions. Revisit selected moments before asking for an explanation.","ask":"What did you see? What can’t the video show about what happens inside?","listen":"Move unobserved explanations to questions or inferences and identify what evidence would help.","source":"375–377","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6}],"cue":{"do":"Record observations and questions from the video before proposing an explanation.","ask":"What did you see, and what can’t the video show about what happens inside?","listen":"An observed action separated from an inference about digestion."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides F–H · Read Termite Guts."},{"title":"Read Termite Guts","ref":"Slides F–H","minutes":"25 min","move":"Read Termite Guts in pairs and label the source of each detail in the evidence organizer.","ask":"What does the article explain that the video could not?","listen":"If students say wood vanishes, have them locate a detail about what happens to some of the material.","source":"378–381","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9}],"cue":{"do":"Read in pairs and label whether each recorded detail came from the reading or the video.","ask":"What can the reading explain that the video alone could not?","listen":"An evidence-backed idea about the role of organisms inside the termite."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides I–J · Build and compare termite models."},{"title":"Build and compare termite models","ref":"Slides I–J","minutes":"35 min","move":"Have students draw the path of material and compare models. Ask them to trace one connection using their organizer.","ask":"Where does the wood start, and where can some of its matter go?","listen":"If they name an outcome without a pathway, connect each arrow to one detail from the reading or video.","source":"380–385","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11}],"cue":{"do":"Have students trace a path of matter in their model and connect it to a recorded source.","ask":"Where does the wood start, and where can some of its matter go?","listen":"A labeled pathway supported by a detail from the video or reading."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides K · Ask about other wood-breakers."},{"title":"Ask about other wood-breakers","ref":"Slides K","minutes":"5 min","move":"Discuss if other organisms in the nurse log system are able to break down the wood of the nurse log.","ask":"What would we need to observe to tell whether something else breaks down wood?","listen":"If students generalize from termites to every organism on the log, carry that claim forward as a question and ask what evidence would distinguish their roles.","source":"386","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-11-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 11 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Save the termite explanation and ask what other organisms might affect the wood.","ask":"What other organism on the log might break down wood?","listen":"A candidate organism and a question to investigate."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":89,"prep":90,"briefing":92,"resume":93,"teaching":94,"teachingEnd":95,"field":96,"checklist":91},"access":"During the evidence sort, invite students to mark each idea as observed in the video or inferred from the reading, then explain one choice orally.","evidence":"Ask students to trace wood, microscopic organisms and termite in a model. If a link is unsupported, have them mark the relevant evidence source and clarify what it establishes.","checklist":["Queue Termites Eating Wood and the optional microscopic view.","Copy Termite Eating Wood and the evidence organizer for each student.","Prepare one Termite Guts reading per pair.","Set out the evidence organizer or publisher-approved notebook alternative before students compare sources."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1CyVpTk56CiCO1kfbl2CXrpcx3ieuGMq4hsUvFEer15s/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 11 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-11.jpg"},{"number":12,"title":"Mushroom evidence","question":"Mushrooms grow on a log. Where does their growth come from?","story":"Termites gave us one way to think about getting matter from wood. Mushrooms raise a different question. Have groups bring different observations together before deciding what those observations can explain. Keep the comparisons handy for Lesson 13, when we’ll trace what happens to matter during decomposition.","science":"Carry forward the termite evidence. Students compare sources to distinguish fungi from plants and animals. Dark growth is evidence against needing light for growth in this case; it does not by itself identify a complete source of matter or energy. Lesson 13 develops the decomposer explanation.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 12\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nMushrooms grow on a log. Where does\ntheir growth come from?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 12\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nTermites gave us one way to think about getting matter from wood. Mushrooms raise a different\nquestion. Have groups bring different observations together before deciding what those observations\ncan explain. Keep the comparisons handy for Lesson 13, when we’ll trace what happens to matter\nduring decomposition.\nHere is the mystery. After investigating termite digestion, students compare evidence about\nmushrooms. Ask them to track what each source shows before comparing how fungi, plants and\nanimals grow.\n1. Recall where we left off | 10 min. Revisit what we figured out about microscopic organisms in our\nlast lesson and recall that next we wanted to explore other organisms in the nurse log system to see if\nthey also break down the nurse log.\n2. Make observations | 30 min. Investigate one data source about mushrooms and then work with a\nmixed group to look for patterns across our findings and compare them to how plants and animals get\nmatter and energy.\n3. Participate in a discussion | 25 min. Share our findings in a Building Understandings Discussion\nand look for patterns in the evidence across groups, to help figure out where mushrooms get matter and\nenergy to grow.\n4. Connect to our communities | 20 min. Individually, we look for mushrooms and related living things\nin our own community and figure out that mushrooms are an example of fungi.\n5. Decide where to go next | 5 min. Determine that we still have not answered our questions about if\nfungi can break down the nurse log, so we decide to start with this next time.\nWhere it leads. The class carries these findings into Lesson 13 to explain where matter goes during\ndecomposition.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 391–393","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 12\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Check mushroom kit lead time and safety guidance. Set up the assigned observation sources and\nkeep them labeled for mixed-group comparisons.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Investigate Mushrooms • Mushrooms in Our Community\nPer small group:\n• Investigate Mushrooms • 1-3 mushrooms • hand lenses • mushroom kits • Data and Instructions for\nExploring Mushrooms • Investigate Mushrooms\nPer class:\n• Driving Question Board • Mushroom Comparison chart\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 394 (PDF viewer p. 400).\nBefore class\nG394, 396, 410 and following setup pages; Student Bundle PDF 103–106 and optional community page\n139. Allow 45 minutes prep and a kit grown at least one week, if using a live kit. Prepare three\nevidence types: close observation (1–3 mushrooms/group), before/after kit or publisher images,\nand light/dark images. Print one source guide per expert group; individual evidence sheets as\ndirected. For about 22, plan six expert groups, two per source, then balanced mixed groups;\nassign extra students as co-reporters. Follow publisher and school handling and cleanup\nprocedures. Confirm the publisher alternate materials if no live kit.\nPrint per student\nInvestigate Mushrooms, Student Bundle PDF 103–106, one set per student (about 22 per class). Hand\nit out before the expert groups collect evidence. For the community activity, use PDF 139 or have\nstudents write directly in their notebooks.\nPrint per expert group\nOne copy of the relevant source instructions per expert group (Teacher Guide printed pp. 412–414).\nUse color if available, or show slides I–J for the corresponding images. Set copies out by source before\nD–J.\nTeacher only\nKeep setup and handling guidance (printed pp. 394, 410–411) and formative guidance (p. 396) handy.\nSave completed source notes for the mixed-group share.","resume":["If K–L cannot start cleanly, pause after D–J.","Source notes and mixed-group comparison sheets.","Begin the discussion. Flex the community activity or schedule an extra block to preserve the evidence comparison."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Reopen fungi questions","ref":"Slides A–C","minutes":"10 min","move":"Revisit what we figured out about microscopic organisms in our last lesson and recall that next we wanted to explore other organisms in the nurse log system to see if they also break down the nurse log.","ask":"What did we observe about mushrooms on the log, and what did we only infer?","listen":"If students equate growing on wood with eating it, record that as a question and ask what evidence about growth would help.","source":"397–398","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2},{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3},{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4}],"cue":{"do":"Return to questions about other organisms on the log, including fungi.","ask":"What do we still need to find out about how mushrooms grow?","listen":"A question about growing conditions or matter and energy."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides D–J · Gather and exchange mushroom evidence."},{"title":"Gather and exchange mushroom evidence","ref":"Slides D–J","minutes":"30 min","move":"Assign expert groups across the three sources. Record observations on Investigate Mushrooms, then regroup with one source represented by each expert. Compare only after students record peers’ evidence.","ask":"Which source did you use? What did you observe?","listen":"If a group lacks a source, have an expert or the teacher provide that source’s recorded observation. Save source notes and comparison sheets at the bell.","source":"399–402","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11}],"cue":{"do":"Assign the three evidence sources to expert groups. Regroup so students record evidence from each source.","ask":"Which source did you use, and what did you observe?","listen":"A specific observation identified by source."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides K–L · Compare patterns across sources."},{"title":"Compare patterns across sources","ref":"Slides K–L","minutes":"25 min","move":"Use the Mushroom Comparison chart to bring claims together. Press for evidence across sources and invite competing interpretations.","ask":"Which result supports that idea? What remains uncertain about matter or energy?","listen":"If students say dark growth proves where matter comes from, separate the evidence about light from the question of matter source.","source":"403–406","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13}],"cue":{"do":"Use the Mushroom Comparison chart to compare claims before summarizing.","ask":"Which result supports that idea, and what remains uncertain?","listen":"Students distinguish evidence about light conditions from evidence about where matter comes from."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides M–O · Connect to community examples."},{"title":"Connect to community examples","ref":"Slides M–O","minutes":"20 min","move":"Use community examples to apply the comparison. Ask students which idea is supported by their observations and which raises a new question.","ask":"How is your example like what we compared?","listen":"Save open questions for decomposition. If time is short, preserve the evidence discussion and flex the community activity.","source":"406–408","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14},{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15},{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Invite students to connect a community example to a source or comparison from the investigation.","ask":"How is your example like what we compared?","listen":"A supported similarity or a new question."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides P · Choose the next fungi question."},{"title":"Choose the next fungi question","ref":"Slides P","minutes":"5 min","move":"Determine that we still have not answered our questions about if fungi can break down the nurse log, so we decide to start with this next time.","ask":"What does our evidence still leave open about whether fungi break down the log?","listen":"If students treat where mushrooms grow as proof of how they get matter, ask what observations or reports would show material changing.","source":"409","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-12-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 12 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17}],"cue":{"do":"Save an unresolved question about whether fungi break down the log.","ask":"What evidence would tell us if fungi break down once-living material?","listen":"A proposed observation or source for Lesson 13."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":97,"prep":98,"briefing":100,"resume":101,"teaching":102,"teachingEnd":103,"field":104,"checklist":99},"access":"During the jigsaw, let a student point to the image or specimen and use a short oral report to name the source, observation, and claim.","evidence":"Ask students to use one observation from their source and a second from a peer. If they repeat a claim without evidence, have them find the recorded feature, condition or comparison that supports it.","checklist":["Start the live mushroom kit at least a week ahead if using it, and verify the image option if needed.","Stage the three evidence sources, group labels, and hand lenses as directed.","Copy Investigate Mushrooms for each student and prepare one source guide per expert group.","Prepare the Mushroom Comparison chart and plan expert-to-mixed group changes and cleanup."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1_CpMaXOefHdB--flqsmGGZS7OfcoSwxJt_VfmbbhGeM/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 12 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-12.jpg"},{"number":13,"title":"Where does it go?","question":"When something decays, where does its matter go?","story":"Last time students compared evidence about mushroom growth. Now we’re following matter as once-living things break down. Ask students to connect observations to their models, then use those ideas to revise the class nurse-log model. Next we’ll test what happens when a new organism changes the web.","science":"Lesson 12 compared fungi with plants and animals. Use the article and time-lapse to model decomposition as matter moving and changing form. The time-lapse alone cannot show all destinations of matter.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 13\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nWhen something decays, where does its\nmatter go?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 13\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nLast time students compared evidence about mushroom growth. Now we’re following matter as\nonce-living things break down. Ask students to connect observations to their models, then use those\nideas to revise the class nurse-log model. Next we’ll test what happens when a new organism changes\nthe web.\nHere is the mystery. Lesson 12 raised questions about what fungi use to grow. Here students use\nreading, a time-lapse and models to explain the role of decomposers in the system.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Recall the questions we had about where mushrooms and other\nfungi get their matter and energy from.\n2. Read an article | 20 min. With a partner, we read an article about mushrooms and other fungi in\ndifferent communities to collect evidence about where they get matter and energy to grow.\n3. Participate in a discussion | 10 min. Discuss what we figured out about mushrooms and other fungi\nfrom reading the article, and categorize them as decomposers.\n4. Make observations | 10 min. Watch a time-lapse video of a decaying flower that includes\ndecomposers and consider what would happen if the system did not include decomposers.\n5. Develop a model to explain | 15 min. In partners, we model to explain how decomposers like\nmushrooms get and use matter and energy.\n6. Revise our model to explain | 20 min. Add new ideas that we have figured out about fungi,\ndecomposers, and their important role as recyclers of matter and energy to our Nurse Log Consensus\nModel.\n7. Update our Growing Ideas charts | 5 min. Individually update our Growing Ideas charts to\ndocument our new ideas about fungi, decomposers, and how matter and energy move through the\nnurse log system.\n8. Generate new ideas | 5 min. With a partner, we discuss what we think happens when other\norganisms besides decomposers are removed from or added to ecosystems.\nWhere it leads. Once the class has modeled these relationships, Lesson 14 asks what changes when\nan organism enters the system.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 417–419","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 13\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Queue the flower decomposition video and prepare the fungi article, evidence collector and class\nmodel.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Communities Celebrate National Mushroom Month newspaper article • Fungi Evidence Collector\nhandout • Notice and Wonder: Decomposers and Matter • Model a Mushroom • My Growing Ideas\nPer partners:\n• Fungi Evidence Collector • Model a Mushroom\nPer class:\n• Flower Decomposition video • Nurse Log Consensus Model chart • previous versions of consensus\nmodel\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 420 (PDF viewer p. 426).\nBefore class\nG420. Prepare the fungi article for each student to use in reading pairs, or share the publisher slide\ndeck. Queue the flower decomposition video, prepare the evidence collector and retrieve the class\nmodel. Student Bundle PDF 107, 109, 111,\n113. Confirm pair and individual copy jobs.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nStudent copies\nOne Fungi Evidence Collector (107), Notice and Wonder: Decomposers and Matter (109), and Model a\nMushroom (111) per student. Students use their own sheets during partner work. Copy My Growing\nIdeas (113) for the close or use notebooks.\nShared reading and teacher prep\nGive each student access to the publisher mushroom-month article for partner reading, using individual\ncopies or the publisher slide deck. Give the evidence collector at the reading, the Notice and Wonder\npage with slide H, and the model sheet at partner modeling. Queue the flower video and have earlier\nclass models available.","resume":["Finish G–J.","Article evidence and time-lapse observations.","Start K with a visible change students can explain, then introduce the model task."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Reopen the fungi evidence","ref":"Slides A–B","minutes":"5 min","move":"Recall the questions we had about where mushrooms and other fungi get their matter and energy from.","ask":"What did our evidence about fungi help explain, and what are we still trying to account for?","listen":"If students use the plant model for fungi without explanation, ask them to point to a finding from the mushroom investigation that the model would need to explain.","source":"424","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2},{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3}],"cue":{"do":"Review students’ mushroom observations and return to the question of how fungi get matter and energy.","ask":"Which part of that question is still open?","listen":"A distinction between evidence about growth conditions and unanswered questions about matter."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides C–D · Read and collect fungi evidence."},{"title":"Read and collect fungi evidence","ref":"Slides C–D","minutes":"20 min","move":"Give each student the Fungi Evidence Collector and access to the mushroom-month article. Have partners read and each record what the source says fungi did.","ask":"What does the article say changed? Which detail supports it?","listen":"If partners give only a name, ask what the fungus did and where the passage explains it.","source":"425–428","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5}],"cue":{"do":"Give each student the evidence collector and access to the mushroom-month article. Read in pairs.","ask":"What does the article say changed? Which detail supports it?","listen":"A named change and the relevant source detail."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides E–F · Discuss the decomposer role."},{"title":"Discuss the decomposer role","ref":"Slides E–F","minutes":"10 min","move":"Discuss what we figured out about mushrooms and other fungi from reading the article, and categorize them as decomposers.","ask":"Which example gives evidence that fungi can break down once-living material?","listen":"If students name fungi without explaining what they do, return to one article detail and ask them to describe the change it reports.","source":"426–429","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7}],"cue":{"do":"Bring evidence from the article together before naming the decomposer role.","ask":"What pattern in the examples helps explain what fungi do?","listen":"A pattern of breaking down once-living material tied to examples in the text."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides G–J · Observe the decomposition time-lapse."},{"title":"Observe the decomposition time-lapse","ref":"Slides G–J","minutes":"10 min","move":"Collect visible changes from the flower time-lapse before asking where matter went. Revisit a useful moment as directed.","ask":"What changed that we could see? What can’t the video track?","listen":"If students say matter is gone, record it as an idea to evaluate. Save observations alongside article evidence.","source":"429–432","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11}],"cue":{"do":"Record visible changes in the flower video before discussing destinations of matter.","ask":"What changed that we could see? What can’t the video track?","listen":"A visible change and a limit of what the recording shows."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides K · Model a mushroom’s matter pathway."},{"title":"Model a mushroom’s matter pathway","ref":"Slides K","minutes":"15 min","move":"Have pairs develop a Model a Mushroom explanation. Use the Fungi Evidence Collector to check one connection before the class model discussion.","ask":"What is one connection in your model that you can explain with evidence from our reading or observations?","listen":"If students draw the mushroom beside the log without a connection, ask them to show what is being broken down and trace where some matter could go. Have them distinguish their inference from what they observed.","source":"433–434","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Have partners label where matter starts and where it can go in Model a Mushroom.","ask":"Which arrow can you connect to the article or time-lapse?","listen":"An arrow with a source and possible destination, tied to evidence."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides L–N · Revise the nurse-log model."},{"title":"Revise the nurse-log model","ref":"Slides L–N","minutes":"20 min","move":"Compare Model a Mushroom explanations and revise the class model. Trace a pathway students can support.","ask":"What is being broken down? Where might some of its matter go?","listen":"If a model ends at disappearance, have students draw a possible destination and label the inference.","source":"435–440","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13},{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14},{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15}],"cue":{"do":"Compare the mushroom models. Revise a pathway in the class model from evidence.","ask":"Where might some of the broken-down material go?","listen":"A possible destination, with inferences identified as such."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides O · Record a decomposition idea."},{"title":"Record a decomposition idea","ref":"Slides O","minutes":"5 min","move":"Individually update our Growing Ideas charts to document our new ideas about fungi, decomposers, and how matter and energy move through the nurse log system.","ask":"What can you now explain about where some matter goes when something decomposes?","listen":"If the entry stops at “it breaks down,” invite a labeled arrow showing one possible pathway and a source that supports the idea.","source":"441","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Have students update Growing Ideas with a change in their explanation.","ask":"How does your new idea explain where some of the matter goes?","listen":"A description of movement or change rather than disappearance."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides P · Ask what happens when a web changes."},{"title":"Ask what happens when a web changes","ref":"Slides P","minutes":"5 min","move":"With a partner, we discuss what we think happens when other organisms besides decomposers are removed from or added to ecosystems.","ask":"If this organism were added to the system, which connection might change?","listen":"If students predict every part will change in the same way, have them name one existing connection and describe how it might be affected first.","source":"442","id":8,"slides":[{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-13-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 13 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17}],"cue":{"do":"Have partners consider what might happen if another organism were added or removed.","ask":"Which connection in our model might change first?","listen":"A named relationship that can motivate the next investigation."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":105,"prep":106,"briefing":108,"resume":109,"teaching":110,"teachingEnd":111,"field":112,"checklist":107},"access":"During partner modeling, offer arrows and labels or oral rehearsal to show where matter starts and where it goes.","evidence":"Ask students to annotate one model arrow and point to article or video evidence. If they infer an unobserved destination, mark that as an inference and ask which source supports it.","checklist":["Prepare the mushroom-month article for each student’s use during paired reading, or share the publisher slide deck.","Queue the flower decomposition time-lapse and gather the earlier class models.","Copy Fungi Evidence Collector, Notice and Wonder, and Model a Mushroom.","Set out the comparison and modeling space. Prepare Growing Ideas or notebooks."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1btxq0AgswQbi4Z-32dcM2_R_7txdb0brhtZTD72En5g/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 13 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-13.jpg"},{"number":14,"title":"Bullfrog interruption","question":"A bullfrog enters the food web. What changes?","story":"Our class model now connects producers, consumers, and decomposers. The game gives students a way to see those connections in action. Compare the web before and after the bullfrog enters. Keep that reasoning available as students move to a new ecosystem in the final lesson.","science":"The class has traced matter and energy through the nurse-log system. The game makes connections visible and supports reasoning about a disruption. Game changes represent possible effects and need interpretation.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 14\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nA bullfrog enters the food web. What\nchanges?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 14\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nOur class model now connects producers, consumers, and decomposers. The game gives students a\nway to see those connections in action. Compare the web before and after the bullfrog enters. Keep that\nreasoning available as students move to a new ecosystem in the final lesson.\nHere is the mystery. Students have traced matter and energy through parts of the nurse-log system.\nThe game lets them represent those connections and think through what happens when the web\nchanges.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Recall what we figured out about the nurse log system in our last\nlesson and start thinking about which component of the nurse log system is the most important.\n2. Play and investigate | 35 min. Each take on the role of one of the components of the nurse log\nsystem while playing a game that helps us visualize a balanced food web.\n3. Read an infographic | 15 min. Read an infographic about American bullfrogs.\n4. Play and investigate | 20 min. Play the Matter and Energy Flow game with the addition of the\nAmerican bullfrog.\n5. Reflect on our work | 10 min. Individually, we use an exit slip to think through our ideas about how\nthe American bullfrog impacts the nurse log food web.\n6. Revisit our Growing Ideas charts | 5 min. Individually, we add what we figured out about how the\nbalance and health of a food web can be disrupted to our Growing Ideas charts.\nWhere it leads. Save the bullfrog predictions and evidence. Lesson 15 takes the food-web ideas into a\ndifferent ecosystem.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 444–446","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 14\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Prepare the game cards, two yarn colors and clips. Set aside the bullfrog card until the publisher calls\nfor its introduction.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• 2-3 clothespins or paper clips • Food Web Exit Slip • My Growing Ideas\nPer small group:\n• 6 Facts About Bullfrogs • American bullfrog game card from the Energy and Matter Flow Game Cards\nreference\nPer class:\n• Class set of the Energy and Matter Flow Game Cards reference • components of the nurse log system\nchart • ball of yellow yarn • ball of yarn of any other color\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 447 (PDF viewer p. 453).\nBefore class\nG447 and Game Guidance. Prepare one game card per student, 2–3 clips each, two yarn colors,\ncomponents chart, infographic and bullfrog card. Keep the introduction card aside until instructed. Plan\nspace and reset for both sections; keep exit sheets separate. Student Bundle PDF 115–117.\nPrint and handout map\nStudent Bundle viewer pages unless marked Teacher Guide printed. Prepare one per student unless\notherwise stated (about 22 per class).\nGame copies\nPrepare one role card per student from the publisher game set. English base cards are in guide printed\npp. 488–502, extra roles pp. 507 onward. Set aside the bullfrog cards (pp. 503–506). Prepare one 6\nFacts About Bullfrogs infographic and bullfrog card per group for K–L. Introduce the role in Round 2.\nStudent and teacher copies\nOne Food Web Exit Slip (115–116) per student for individual work at the end. Use My Growing Ideas\n(117) for P or continue in notebooks. Keep Game Guidance (printed pp. 463–465) at the teacher\nstation.","resume":["Before introducing the bullfrog, pause if needed.","A sketch or photo of the baseline web and the infographic prediction if started.","Resume K–L before introducing the bullfrog. Reconstruct only the connections needed for comparison."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Revisit the nurse-log system","ref":"Slides A–B","minutes":"5 min","move":"Recall what we figured out about the nurse log system in our last lesson and start thinking about which component of the nurse log system is the most important.","ask":"What would change for another organism if this connection disappeared?","listen":"If students identify a component without a relationship, point to the class model and trace one path into or out of it.","source":"450","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":2},{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":3}],"cue":{"do":"Reopen the class model and invite students to name connections they think matter.","ask":"Which component depends on another one, and how?","listen":"A relationship between named parts of the system."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides C–J · Play the baseline web game."},{"title":"Play the baseline web game","ref":"Slides C–J","minutes":"35 min","move":"Assign baseline roles and use the yarn colors according to Game Guidance. Capture the starting web before adding a species.","ask":"What does this strand represent? Which two parts does it connect?","listen":"If students reverse the relationship, name source and receiver and trace it. Save a photo or sketch before resetting.","source":"451–457","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":4},{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":8},{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":10},{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":11}],"cue":{"do":"Assign baseline roles and use the yarn colors as directed. Save the starting web before introducing the bullfrog.","ask":"What does this strand represent, and which two parts does it connect?","listen":"A correctly traced relationship with a named source and receiver."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides K–L · Use the bullfrog infographic."},{"title":"Use the bullfrog infographic","ref":"Slides K–L","minutes":"15 min","move":"Give groups the infographic and bullfrog card. Have them use evidence to complete the card and predict a change before Round 2.","ask":"Which fact supports your prediction?","listen":"If the prediction is vague, ask students to name one connection and the organism affected. Keep the card aside until Round 2.","source":"458","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":12},{"id":"L","label":"Slide L","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-l.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":13}],"cue":{"do":"Give groups the infographic and bullfrog card. Have them use evidence to predict a change.","ask":"Which fact supports your prediction?","listen":"A prediction about a named organism or connection supported by the infographic."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides M–N · Add the bullfrog and compare."},{"title":"Add the bullfrog and compare","ref":"Slides M–N","minutes":"20 min","move":"At M, add one bullfrog player. After the first chain forms, pause at N to connect the frog’s removal and the addition of more bullfrogs to the infographic. Compare the resulting yarn paths with the baseline.","ask":"Who has more yarn now, who has less, and what does that tell us about energy flow in this model?","listen":"If students call the web healthy because the bullfrogs have plenty of yarn, ask them to point to an organism with fewer connections than before and trace what changed. Check their reasoning about matter flow before deciding whether to replay the matter round.","source":"459–460","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"M","label":"Slide M","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-m.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":14},{"id":"N","label":"Slide N","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-n.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":15}],"cue":{"do":"Introduce the bullfrog in Round 2 and compare a changed pathway with the baseline.","ask":"Which connection changed first, and what might be affected next?","listen":"A direct effect followed by a reasoned indirect effect."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides O · Collect the individual exit slip."},{"title":"Collect the individual exit slip","ref":"Slides O","minutes":"10 min","move":"Give the Food Web Exit Slip for individual work. Save discussion and feedback until after collection.","ask":"After collection, ask: Which relationship changed, and what does your model show about matter and energy flow?","listen":"If a response only says “the bullfrog made it unhealthy,” look for a named relationship and an explanation that more matter and energy went to bullfrogs. Use that gap to plan follow-up.","source":"461","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"O","label":"Slide O","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-o.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":16}],"cue":{"do":"Give students the Food Web Exit Slip and have them respond individually.","ask":"Use the publisher exit-slip prompt and clarify directions as needed.","listen":"After collection, look for a supported change in a specific web relationship."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides P · Record what a disruption changes."},{"title":"Record what a disruption changes","ref":"Slides P","minutes":"5 min","move":"Individually, we add what we figured out about how the balance and health of a food web can be disrupted to our Growing Ideas charts.","ask":"What did the game help explain about how adding a species can change a food web?","listen":"If students write only that the bullfrog is harmful, ask them to use a specific game relationship to explain how matter or energy flow changed.","source":"462","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"P","label":"Slide P","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-14-p.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 14 Slides · CC BY-NC 4.0","deckPosition":17}],"cue":{"do":"Have students update Growing Ideas and save a connection to bring into the grassland lesson.","ask":"How did adding one organism affect another part of the web?","listen":"A change traced through a specific feeding relationship."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":113,"prep":114,"briefing":116,"resume":117,"teaching":118,"teachingEnd":119,"field":120,"checklist":115},"access":"After the game and before the independent exit slip, let students trace one yarn connection and explain a direct effect before discussing indirect effects.","evidence":"Use the exit response to check for a direct or indirect effect supported by a connection. If the response names only an organism, ask the student to describe the link and what changed.","checklist":["Set out one role card and two or three clips per student, plus two colors of yarn.","Set aside the bullfrog cards and infographic for the publisher’s later step.","Prepare the components chart and individual Food Web Exit Slips.","Plan how to capture the baseline web and reset the game for the next section."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1vtgvpeK0eqy_fRpPemKfA5DMJYaJLgSuAMUI8YmavNE/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 14 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-14.jpg"},{"number":15,"title":"Grassland connections","question":"Can the nurse-log ideas explain a grassland?","story":"We’ve used the nurse log to trace matter and energy and to reason about a disruption. Now students get a chance to use those same ideas in a grassland. Let the food-chain practice prepare them for the independent task, then use their work to see what transfers to the new system.","science":"Use Lesson 14's bullfrog disruption to bridge to a new ecosystem. Matter moves among organisms and the environment; energy enters through producers and transfers through feeding. Keep class practice and the individual wild-hog transfer task separate.","briefing":"SCIENCE STUDIO  /  FIELD BROADCAST 15\nTHE SCIENCE BRIEFING\nCan the nurse-log ideas explain a\ngrassland?\nHOST SEGMENT  /  UNIT 5.1  /  LESSON 15\nWHERE WE ARE IN THE STORY\nWe’ve used the nurse log to trace matter and energy and to reason about a disruption. Now students\nget a chance to use those same ideas in a grassland. Let the food-chain practice prepare them for the\nindependent task, then use their work to see what transfers to the new system.\nHere is the mystery. Students know how organisms in the nurse-log system are connected. Today\nthey transfer those ideas to a grassland and explain how matter and energy move through its food web.\n1. Recall where we left off | 5 min. Look back on what we figured out from the Matter and Energy Flow\ngame.\n2. Revise our Class Consensus Model to explain | 10 min. Add the ideas we figured out about\nAmerican bullfrogs being added to the nurse log system to our Class Consensus Model.\n3. Read a book | 10 min. Read Ecosystems Explorers and discuss the different ecosystems we\nobserved.\n4. Identify related phenomena | 15 min. In pairs, we predict and discuss where organisms in a\ngrassland ecosystem get and give matter and energy.\n5. Make and compare predictions | 20 min. Create grassland food chains and communicate our food\nchains to the class.\n6. Apply ideas to another phenomenon | 25 min. Individually, we develop a model of what happens\nwhen a wild hog is introduced to a grassland ecosystem.\n7. Consider what is next | 5 min. Celebrate our learning and look forward to learning more.\nWhere it leads. Use the publisher’s transfer task to see how students apply the ideas in a new setting.\nKeep their work separate from teacher scoring guidance.\nOPENSCIED UNIT 5.1 / TEACHER GUIDE PRINTED PP. 510–512","prep":"PREP DESK  /  LESSON 15\nSetup checklist\nSET UP REQUIRED\nDO BEFORE CLASS\n• Prepare the grassland organism cards by pair. Queue Ecosystem Explorers and separate the student\nassessment from scoring materials.\nPUBLISHER MATERIALS\nPer student:\n• Grassland Ecosystem Food Chains • Grassland Ecosystem\nPer partners:\n• Grassland Ecosystem Organism Cards\nPer small group:\n• Class Consensus Model\nPer class:\n• Ecosystem Explorers book\nSource: Teacher Guide printed p. 513 (PDF viewer p. 519).\nBefore class\nG513, 515 and 529 onward; S119 food chains, S121–125 organism cards, S151–155 assessment. Print\none card set per pair (about 11/class), cut and fold as directed; prepare bullfrog cutouts and tape,\nmarkers, prior class model and Ecosystem Explorers. Print the food chains and assessment per student\nas assigned. Keep scoring guidance teacher-only. Check the guide and school conditions before giving\nany supports during assessment.\nPrint practice materials\nOne Grassland Ecosystem Organism Cards set per pair (Student Bundle PDF 121–125, about 11 sets\nper class). Cut on solid lines and fold on dotted lines before D–G. Print one Food Chains handout per\nstudent (PDF 119) for H–I. Prepare the bullfrog cutouts from Teacher Guide printed p. 527.\nHold for slide J\nOne Grassland Ecosystem assessment per student (Student Bundle PDF 151–155, about 22 sets per\nclass). Keep it separate from practice. Read the scenario and take task questions as directed. Reserve\n30 minutes of independent work per printed p. 525. The snapshot lists 25 minutes for J, so schedule\nextra time if needed.\nTeacher only\nKeep the scoring guide (printed pp. 529–531) with teacher materials. Use it after collecting student\nwork. Confirm school-required accommodations and the publisher directions before administration.","resume":["If practice runs long, schedule a separate assessment window.","Card arrangement or sketch and the last completed H–I step.","Protect the full J window next block, including 30 minutes of independent work."],"earlyResume":"","stages":[{"title":"Revisit the food-web game","ref":"Slides A","minutes":"5 min","move":"Look back on what we figured out from the Matter and Energy Flow game.","ask":"What changed in the yarn web after the bullfrog entered?","listen":"If students remember the bullfrog but not its effect, reopen the baseline and Round 2 evidence and have them compare one path.","source":"516","id":1,"slides":[{"id":"A","label":"Slide A","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-a.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":2}],"cue":{"do":"Reopen the baseline and bullfrog evidence from the previous lesson.","ask":"What did the second round show about changing one part of a food web?","listen":"A direct change linked to an observed game connection."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides B · Revise the nurse-log consensus model."},{"title":"Revise the nurse-log consensus model","ref":"Slides B","minutes":"10 min","move":"Add the ideas we figured out about American bullfrogs being added to the nurse log system to our Class Consensus Model.","ask":"Where could we add a bullfrog connection, and what supports it?","listen":"If students place it in the model without a relationship, ask which organism it interacts with and what the infographic or game showed.","source":"517–519","id":2,"slides":[{"id":"B","label":"Slide B","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-b.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":3}],"cue":{"do":"Add the supported bullfrog relationships to the class model.","ask":"Which relationship needs to change in our model, and what supports it?","listen":"A revision linked to evidence from the game or infographic."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides C · Read about other ecosystems."},{"title":"Read about other ecosystems","ref":"Slides C","minutes":"10 min","move":"Read Ecosystems Explorers and discuss the different ecosystems we observed.","ask":"Which relationship from the nurse-log model could also occur in a different ecosystem?","listen":"If students expect the same organisms everywhere, have them name a different organism or setting from the book and identify a similar type of interaction.","source":"520–521","id":3,"slides":[{"id":"C","label":"Slide C","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-c.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":4}],"cue":{"do":"Use Ecosystem Explorers to compare settings before introducing grassland organisms.","ask":"What looks different, and which kinds of relationships might still be similar?","listen":"A comparison of settings without assuming identical organisms."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides D–G · Predict using the grassland cards."},{"title":"Predict using the grassland cards","ref":"Slides D–G","minutes":"15 min","move":"Introduce the grassland setting. Have partners inspect the organism cards before arranging relationships.","ask":"What does this card say the organism gets or gives?","listen":"If students assume a nurse-log relationship carries over, send them to the grassland card for evidence.","source":"521–522","id":4,"slides":[{"id":"D","label":"Slide D","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-d.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":5},{"id":"E","label":"Slide E","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-e.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":6},{"id":"F","label":"Slide F","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-f.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":7},{"id":"G","label":"Slide G","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-g.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":8}],"cue":{"do":"Have partners read organism cards before arranging their relationships.","ask":"What does this card say the organism gets or gives?","listen":"A proposed relationship supported by card evidence."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides H–I · Build and compare food chains."},{"title":"Build and compare food chains","ref":"Slides H–I","minutes":"20 min","move":"Use the Food Chains handout for practice. Compare arrangements and have students explain arrows before the separate transfer task.","ask":"Which organism is the source, which is the receiver, and what moves?","listen":"If an arrow is reversed, have students read the relationship aloud using the cards. Save a sketch and the last finished step if pausing.","source":"523–524","id":5,"slides":[{"id":"H","label":"Slide H","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-h.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":9},{"id":"I","label":"Slide I","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-i.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":10}],"cue":{"do":"Use the Food Chains handout for practice. Ask students to explain arrows before the independent task.","ask":"Which organism is the source, which is the receiver, and what moves?","listen":"An arrow interpreted in the correct direction with a named transfer."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides J · Administer the grassland assessment."},{"title":"Administer the grassland assessment","ref":"Slides J","minutes":"30 min independent work, plus directions","move":"Distribute the assessment at J, read the scenario, and take task questions. Keep the 30-minute independent work window continuous. Follow the guide’s access options and required accommodations.","ask":"Clarify directions as permitted. Keep practice examples and scoring notes separate.","listen":"Use the scoring guide after collection to plan follow-up on transfer. Keep instructional feedback for after the task.","source":"525","id":6,"slides":[{"id":"J","label":"Slide J","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-j.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":11}],"cue":{"do":"Distribute the official task at J and read the scenario. Reserve 30 minutes for independent work.","ask":"Invite questions about the task directions within the publisher’s guidance.","listen":"After collection, look for transfer to a new ecosystem using the official scoring guide."},"transition":"Next in the publisher sequence: Slides K · Close the unit."},{"title":"Close the unit","ref":"Slides K","minutes":"5 min","move":"Celebrate our learning and look forward to learning more.","ask":"Which idea from this unit could help you explain an ecosystem you have not studied?","listen":"If students give a broad takeaway, invite them to name a relationship and describe how they could use it in a different place.","source":"526","id":7,"slides":[{"id":"K","label":"Slide K","path":"resources/slide-previews/lesson-15-k.png","credit":"OpenSciEd · Unit 5.1 Lesson 15 Slides · CC BY 4.0","deckPosition":12}],"cue":{"do":"Recognize students’ learning and invite them to name an idea they can use again.","ask":"What can you explain now about how matter or energy moves in an ecosystem?","listen":"A reflection connected to a model, observation, or investigation."},"transition":"Close the lesson and use Pause / Resume to record what to carry forward."}],"pages":{"divider":121,"prep":122,"briefing":124,"resume":125,"teaching":126,"teachingEnd":127,"field":128,"checklist":123},"access":"During the food-chain practice before slide J, have students point to the source and receiver for one arrow and explain what moves. Keep this rehearsal separate from the independent task.","evidence":"Before J, check one chain for arrow direction and whether students can describe matter or energy transfer. During J, preserve independent work. Use the official rubric, record what the model shows, and follow the guide for individual clarification when needed.","checklist":["Prepare grassland organism card sets by pair and cut or fold them as directed.","Bring the class consensus model, Ecosystem Explorers, markers, and the bullfrog cutouts.","Copy Grassland Ecosystem Food Chains for practice.","Set aside the individual assessment for slide J and keep scoring guidance teacher-only. Reserve the full independent work window."],"slideResource":{"url":"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1aExHm4h4vn0eJXEXTdC5R3ZGOORdh1EBIVJ5NDVpRWo/edit?authuser=1","label":"Open Lesson 15 school-account deck"},"art":"art/crt-lesson-15.jpg"}];
